{"timestamp":"2026-10-24T14:56:03.022Z","ordinal":294258,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a16e62-7365-74b3-a660-6c02335c1b7f","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-016feb16-e5c3-4ec9-ab70-c5167093d331","process_id":"6027","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","git show origin/main:docs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md | nl -ba | sed -n '110,190p'; git show origin/main:eval/README.md | nl -ba | sed -n '1,220p'; git show origin/main:docs/v1.0.0/README.md | sed -n '1,90p'"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"git show origin/main:docs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md | nl -ba | sed -n '110,190p'; git show origin/main:eval/README.md | nl -ba | sed -n '1,220p'; git show origin/main:docs/v1.0.0/README.md | sed -n '1,90p'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"   110\t| ACC-TUI-STEER | PTY: submit a task; while a tool runs, type steering text and press Enter; also queue a steer before a cancel | Steering is applied at the next step boundary. Unapplied steering is returned to the composer. Nothing is dropped silently (existing `sidecar_steering_diff` plus PTY s6). |\n   111\t| ACC-TUI-A11Y | PTY with `NO_COLOR=1` and with `TERM=dumb` at 40×14 and 100×30 | Zero colour SGR codes. Every state is distinguishable by glyph and word. Hints are complete at 40 columns (TUI-10). |\n   112\t| ACC-SEC-JAIL | `core/tests` plus harness-core calls with `..`, absolute, symlinked-file, symlinked-dir and symlinked-parent payloads | Every one is refused (C §4 R1 set). |\n   113\t| ACC-SEC-B6 | Re-run `docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/repro/zz_trust_repro.test.ts`. Seeded-secret scan of `~/.nexus` and `.nexus` after a scripted turn. | Zero plaintext seeded secrets in durable stores or outbound bodies. |\n   114\t| ACC-PRV-LIVE | `nexus providers verify --provider <p> -m <pinned model> -j` for each provider listed as supported (DEC-06) | `passed: true`. Receipts archived. |\n   115\t| ACC-PRV-STANDALONE | §4.2 on the installed RC through the approved stripped proxy, with gateway credentials absent from the child environment | Every required profile completes through the proxy and the absent-gateway boundary holds; receipts archived. |\n   116\t| ACC-PLAT | E2 matrix, §7 | All rows pass. |\n   117\t| ACC-QUAL-T2 | §6.1 + §6.4 | Thresholds met. |\n   118\t| ACC-QUAL-T3 | §6.3 + §6.4 | Thresholds met. |\n   119\t| ACC-QUAL-T4 | §6.2 + §6.4 | Thresholds met. |\n   120\t| ACC-QUAL-SMOKE | `make test` includes the T1 self-test and the T4 smoke | Present and passing. |\n   121\t| ACC-TUI-MANUAL | §5.3 on the installed RC across five required terminal environments and three required sizes | Every cell passes; screenshots and per-cell verdicts are archived. |\n   122\t\n   123\t## 4. Standalone and provider qualification (REQ-PRV-106, REQ-PRV-111, REQ-QUAL-107)\n   124\t\n   125\t### 4.1 Clean-HOME procedure\n   126\t\n   127\t1. `export HOME=$(mktemp -d)`.\n   128\t2. Install the RC with the documented command.\n   129\t3. Create `~/.config/nexus/config.env` containing only:\n   130\t   - `LOCAL_LLM_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:<proxy>/v1`\n   131\t   - `LOCAL_LLM_MODEL=<id as listed by the proxy>`\n   132\t   - `LOCAL_LLM_API_KEY=REDACTED\n   133\t\n   134\t   No gateway token file, no homelab paths, no `NODE_*` overrides.\n   135\t4. Start `node eval/standalone/plain_proxy.mjs --upstream <gateway> --token-file <operator token> [--emulate <kind>]`. The gateway token stays with the proxy and never reaches the Nexus HOME.\n   136\t5. Run `nexus doctor --json` and archive it. It must report the server kind and context source with no `gateway` capability.\n   137\t\n   138\t### 4.2 Standalone matrix\n   139\t\n   140\t| Profile | What Nexus sees | Runs | Pass condition |\n   141\t|---|---|---|---|\n   142\t| P-plain | Bare OpenAI-compatible `/models` and chat. No tokenize, no capacity headers. | Standalone T2 subset (≥ 10 held-out tasks) × 3 runs; T4 classes 1, 3, 4, 7 × 3 | T2 correctness ≥ (gateway-direct T2 correctness on the same tasks − 10 pts). False VERIFIED = 0. T4 truthful = 100%. Context source reported as `client_default` or an operator override, with no error. |\n   143\t| P-llamacpp | Emulated llama.cpp `/props` and overflow body | Same tasks × 1 run; plus the forced-overflow case (context limit set to 8k) | Context discovered from `/props`. Overflow gives one compact-and-retry, then success or an actionable error. |\n   144\t| P-vllm | Emulated `max_model_len` and vLLM overflow body | Same as P-llamacpp | Same, with the context source `max_model_len` |\n   145\t| P-ollama | Emulated `/api/show` and Ollama overflow body | Same as P-llamacpp | Same, with the context source `ollama_show` |\n   146\t| Conformance | CI fixtures for llama.cpp, vLLM, LM Studio and Ollama shapes | CI | All pass (ACC-INF-01, -02, -05) |\n   147\t\n   148\tACC-INF-10 passes only when every row passes. The release notes state the scope in the form required by DEC-16.\n   149\t\n   150\t### 4.3 Provider matrix (E5)\n   151\t\n   152\t| Provider | Tier (DEC-06) | Required for v1.0.0 |\n   153\t|---|---|---|\n   154\t| OpenRouter, pinned model | Supported | `providers verify` PASS, plus one live small task (a §6-style bug fix) reaching a correct outcome |\n   155\t| ChatGPT sign-in | Experimental | Verify PASS recorded (already PASS at db1ca55; repeat on the RC) |\n   156\t| OpenAI API key, Anthropic | Experimental unless keys are added | If promoted: verify PASS plus one live task. Otherwise NOT RUN, and they are labelled experimental. |\n   157\t| Copilot | Experimental | Not required |\n   158\t\n   159\t## 5. Terminal acceptance (REQ-QUAL-108)\n   160\t\n   161\t### 5.1 CI PTY suites (E1)\n   162\t\n   163\tThese must pass:\n   164\t\n   165\t- `tui_approval_input_e2e`\n   166\t- `tui_diff_e2e`\n   167\t- `tui_maturity_e2e`\n   168\t- `tui_model_picker_e2e`\n   169\t- `tui_multiline_e2e`\n   170\t- `tui_surface_e2e`\n   171\t- new: `repl_approval_e2e`\n   172\t- new: `process_lifecycle_e2e`\n   173\t- new: `startup_unreachable_e2e`\n   174\t- new: the package PTY smoke\n   175\t\n   176\t### 5.2 Scripted PTY scenarios against the installed RC (E3, mock gateway)\n   177\t\n   178\tThese are ports of `evidence/D_pty/`.\n   179\t\n   180\t| Scenario | Pass condition |\n   181\t|---|---|\n   182\t| s1 startup, unreachable endpoint | The endpoint is named, with a fix hint. The turn ends with guidance in ≤ 2.5 min. |\n   183\t| s2 approval under continuous typing, paste and burst, at 40×14 | No decision is made by stray input. A deliberate key decides. |\n   184\t| s3 completion | The outcome is shown in words: VERIFIED, REGRESSION_FREE or UNVERIFIED. |\n   185\t| s4–s6 cancel and steer | Cancel is acknowledged within 1 frame and idle within 5 s. Steering is applied. |\n   186\t| s7 resume | The prior conversation is shown. |\n   187\t| s8–s9 cancel or quit during a tool | Zero surviving processes. |\n   188\t| r1a / r1d REPL approvals | DENIED for a lone Enter and for a typed-ahead `y`. |\n   189\t\n   190\t### 5.3 Manual real-terminal matrix (E3)\n     1\t# Evaluation harness (qualification assessment §9)\n     2\t\n     3\t`run.mjs` runs tasks against one agent configuration and appends a JSON line per run; `score.mjs` reports the scores\n     4\tseparately (never combined), per task and configuration, with 95 % Wilson intervals.\n     5\t\n     6\t`docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md` is a historical checkpoint at source revision `6151a23`; use this file and the current\n     7\trunner source for evaluator behavior added after that revision. Neither document represents completed M5 qualification.\n     8\t\n     9\t```bash\n    10\tnode eval/run.mjs --config eval/configs/gateway-engineering-deep.json --out eval/out/<label> [--only id,id] [--runs N]\n    11\tnode eval/score.mjs eval/out/<label>/results.jsonl          # markdown table; --json for machine output\n    12\tnode eval/score.mjs --pairs \"$NEXUS_RESULTS\" \"$GOOSE_RESULTS\" \"$OPENCODE_RESULTS\"\n    13\t```\n    14\t\n    15\tEach run uses a frozen copy of the task tree, a fresh workspace and `HOME`, and a unique output directory. The agent\n    16\treceives only its workspace, isolated home, selected read-only runtime files, and a run-scoped token for a local proxy;\n    17\tthe real gateway credential stays in the harness process. The candidate runs in Bubblewrap user, PID and network\n    18\tnamespaces, with nested user namespaces disabled. Legacy development tasks may still use `hidden: { dir, into, command }`;\n    19\tthose commands execute against the candidate workspace after the agent exits and are **not safe for qualification**, because\n    20\tvalidator code can inspect or modify files in that workspace. Qualification requires `hidden: { protocol: \"blackbox-v1\",\n    21\tcasesFile, runtime, argv, timeoutMs, totalTimeoutMs, maxOutputBytes }`. Its private JSON cases contain only `input` and\n    22\t`expected`; the host evaluator keeps expected values in its process and launches the candidate runtime separately for each\n    23\tinput using a fresh top-level Bubblewrap boundary. The candidate receives one input on stdin and returns one JSON value on\n    24\tstdout. Its workspace is read-only; private oracle files are not mounted. Candidate output and diagnostics are discarded,\n    25\tand result logs contain only case counts and failed indexes. Supported runtimes are explicitly allowlisted (`node`,\n    26\t`python3`); the only argument is a validated relative candidate entrypoint (no runtime flags, eval expressions, or extra\n    27\targuments), and per-case/aggregate time and output bounds are enforced. Oracle files must be outside the copied fixture.\n    28\tAbsolute paths, traversal, symlinks and unsupported fixture entries are rejected. `manifest.json` records the harness source-file hashes, model fingerprint, corpus/task\n    29\thashes when qualification is configured, executable version, budgets, host and Node version. Each result records the\n    30\texecutable version and effective step/minute budget; per-run logs go to `logs/<task>-<run>/`. The runner refuses an\n    31\texisting output directory so a retry cannot append to or replace prior evidence.\n    32\t\n    33\tQualification runs require `nexusExecutable` to name the absolute path of the installed RC,\n    34\t`expectedAgentVersion` to pin its version, and `nexusPackageSha256` to record the expected release-asset digest from checksum verification. The runner checks `nexus --version` and refuses the checkout\n    35\t`cli/dist/index.js` or a version mismatch before starting an agent. Peer versions are read using each adapter's\n    36\t`--version` command inside the same Bubblewrap filesystem/network boundary, with a cleared environment and bounded\n    37\toutput/time; executable paths and SHA-256 hashes are archived alongside the RC package\n    38\tdigest. `score.mjs --pairs` takes exactly the Nexus,\n    39\tGoose and OpenCode result files and refuses mismatched model fingerprints, sampling declarations, context/output\n    40\tlimits, explicit per-task request and aggregate requested-output ceilings, run pairs, executable identities, or\n    41\teffective step/minute budgets. The proxy permits only model-catalog and inference routes, strips client-supplied\n    42\tauthorization and control-plane headers, and enforces configured request/body/output ceilings. These are requested\n    43\tcompletion-token ceilings, not exact usage accounting. It reports peer hidden-test results\n    44\tseparately; scores are never combined. The Nexus adapter passes the recorded temperature and step/minute limits;\n    45\tOpenCode receives a primary evaluation agent with the recorded step limit, temperature and seed; Goose receives the\n    46\tmatching `--max-turns`, context/output limits and temperature settings. Qualification mode currently requires\n    47\t`sampling.temperature` to be `0` across all three adapters; seed metadata remains recorded when configured.\n    48\t\n    49\t## Task format (`eval/tasks/<id>/task.json`)\n    50\t\n    51\t| Field | Meaning |\n    52\t|---|---|\n    53\t| `id`, `tier` | T1 smoke (scripted model, CI), T2 standard, T3 long-task, T4 fault-injected |\n    54\t| `fixture` or `repo` | a directory in the task, or `{ \"url\", \"commit\" }` (external repositories at pinned commits) |\n    55\t| `setup` | shell commands run in the workspace before the agent (e.g. `npm ci`) |\n    56\t| `objective` | the operator text given to the agent |\n    57\t| `steps`, `minutes` | budgets (defaults from the configuration: 300 steps, 120 min) |\n    58\t| `hidden` | `{ dir, into, command, timeoutSec }` — tests the agent never sees; pass = artifact correct |\n    59\t| `regression` | `{ command }` — the repository's own suite after the run |\n    60\t\n    61\t## Scores\n    62\t\n    63\tcompletion claimed · artifact correct (hidden tests) · false VERIFIED (VERIFIED while hidden tests fail) · regressions\n    64\tintroduced · timed out / harness errors · wall time, steps, tokens (median). Context qualifier (`contextNotRetained`) is\n    65\trecorded per run.\n    66\t\n    67\t## Scoring integrity\n    68\t\n    69\t- **Model proxy.** Every agent reaches the model through a per-run forwarder (`eval/model_proxy.mjs`) that `run.mjs`\n    70\t  starts. Each record carries `model: { requests, answered, upstreamFailures, firstRequestAt, firstAnswerAt }`. Set\n    71\t  `\"proxy\": false` in a config to connect agents directly; such runs are then scored only by the legacy rule below.\n    72\t- **Infrastructure reruns** (`eval/infra.mjs`). A run is rerun only when the model never answered *and* the endpoint\n    73\t  was observed failing, or when the harness itself errored. The rule is the same for every agent. A run in which the\n    74\t  model answered is always scored: a crash is a failure (and counts towards the crash rate), not a rerun. Records from\n    75\t  before the proxy fall back to each agent's own connection-failure message, and the script says how many it judged\n    76\t  that way.\n    77\t- **Contamination.** Contaminated runs (the task directory changed, or its path appeared in the agent's output) are\n    78\t  left out of pooled pass rates and counted separately.\n    79\t- **Reruns are listed.** `score.mjs` lists every replaced run with what it showed and what the rerun showed.\n    80\t- **Pooled metrics.** Pooled rows report crash rate, regressions, tokens per correct result and wall seconds per\n    81\t  correct result. The cost of failed runs counts towards these.\n    82\t\n    83\t## Fault injection (T4)\n    84\t\n    85\tA task or config may list `faults: [{ kind, atRequest }]`. The run's model proxy then injects that fault into the Nth\n    86\tchat request (counted from 1):\n    87\t\n    88\t| kind | Fault |\n    89\t|---|---|\n    90\t| `refuse` | The connection drops without an answer |\n    91\t| `overloaded` | 503 with `Retry-After: 1` |\n    92\t| `stream_cut` | The stream is cut after its first tool-call fragment |\n    93\t| `truncate` | `finish_reason` becomes `length` |\n    94\t| `context_overflow` | llama.cpp's HTTP 400 `exceed_context_size_error` |\n    95\t| `malformed_tool_json` | The first tool call's arguments are no longer valid JSON |\n    96\t\n    97\tWhat was injected is recorded in `model.faultsInjected`. Such runs are never treated as infrastructure reruns.\n    98\t`score.mjs` reports, per fault kind:\n    99\t\n   100\t- runs;\n   101\t- **truthful** (the run ended with its own result, did not hang past its budget, and never claimed VERIFIED while the\n   102\t  hidden tests failed);\n   103\t- **recovered** (the hidden tests pass);\n   104\t- hung past budget.\n   105\t\n   106\tThe remaining T4 classes come from task fixtures (a hanging test command, a full disk) and from the SIGTERM-then-resume\n   107\trunner case. `make test` includes a T4 smoke test with two injected faults (`cli/test/eval_harness.test.ts`).\n   108\t\n   109\t## Qualification runs\n   110\t\n   111\tA config with `\"qualification\": true` is the v1.0.0 evidence run (docs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md §6). `run.mjs` refuses to start\n   112\tone, before any agent runs, unless every item below holds:\n   113\t\n   114\t- **Tasks are well formed** (`eval/validate.mjs`): one source (fixture, or a repo pinned to a full commit id), an isolated\n   115\t  `blackbox-v1` hidden oracle, and `class` (bugfix · feature · refactor · debug) and `difficulty` (easy · medium · hard). Development runs check\n   116\t  everything except class and difficulty.\n   117\t- **The model is identified, not just named.** `fingerprint: { weightsSha256, serverBuild }` and\n   118\t  `sampling: { temperature, seed }` are set in the config, and the endpoint lists the model. The fingerprint (with the\n   119\t  listed owner and context) is written to `manifest.json`.\n   120\t- **Hidden tests are out of the agent's reach.** `runAs` names a separate evaluator user. Candidate execution occurs\n   121\t  inside the Bubblewrap mount namespace and does not bind the task store or validator sources; its `HOME`, `/tmp`,\n   122\t  `/run`, and network namespace are isolated. Runtime files are explicit read-only binds. The harness grants the\n   123\t  evaluator UID ACL access only to the disposable workspace, home and per-run proxy directory. Before qualification,\n   124\t  verify the account and these existing operating-system permissions through the approved local setup procedure:\n   125\t  1. `sudo useradd --system --create-home nexus-eval`\n   126\t  2. A narrowly constrained sudoers rule permitting only the required Bubblewrap invocation as `nexus-eval`; do not\n   127\t     grant arbitrary `env` command execution.\n   128\t  3. Keep task stores unreadable to the evaluator account; the runner does not mount them into the candidate namespace.\n   129\t  4. A Nexus install, Node and the peer agents that `nexus-eval` can read and execute.\n   130\t\n   131\t  Development runs may omit `runAs`; Bubblewrap isolation still applies. Qualification is refused without a separate\n   132\t  evaluator account, `setfacl`, explicit `sandboxRuntimeRoots`, and read-only `sandboxRuntimePaths` contained by those\n   133\t  roots. Protected roots such as `/etc`, `/dev`, `/proc`, `/sys`, `/run`, `/var`, `/tmp`, and sensitive user-cache paths\n   134\t  are rejected. No setup procedure is applied by the runner.\n   135\t\n   136\t- **Repository workspaces carry no history beyond the pinned commit.** `eval/workspace.mjs` stages a repo task as a\n   137\t  fresh `git init` that fetches only the pinned commit and its ancestors from the local cache (`--no-tags`, no\n   138\t  FETCH_HEAD), then checks out that commit detached. There is no `--shared` clone, alternates link, remote, tag or\n   139\t  descendant object, so the upstream fix that follows a held-out baseline is not reachable from the workspace. Staging\n   140\t  verifies this and refuses the run otherwise.\n   141\t\n   142\t- **Corpus binding is integrity evidence, not approval authentication.** The qualification config pins the exact\n   143\t  corpus-manifest SHA-256, and the run manifest repeats the selected task hashes. The v1 manifest binds every task's\n   144\t  full task tree in row `sha256`. The heldout-v2 manifest binds each task's raw `task.json` and hidden-directory digest for\n   145\t  pinned-repository tasks; a local fixture additionally requires a full-tree `sha256` because those two v2 digests do\n   146\t  not cover fixture files. The frozen config carries `corpusCustody: { custodian, approvedAt, reportPath, reportSha256 }`\n   147\t  and may also name `storeRoot`, `validatorPath`, `referencePath`, and `authorLogPath`; the report path and optional\n   148\t  private paths are protected from runtime mounts. These values and the manifest's custody labels are claims, not\n   149\t  signatures. Independent custody acceptance, pre-RC commit timing, and configuration freeze must be verified and\n   150\t  archived by the acceptance reviewer before any qualification run. The current candidate task package has not been\n   151\t  independently accepted.\n   152\t\n   153\t### Isolation limits\n   154\t\n   155\tBubblewrap plus the current runner enforces separate mount/user/PID/network namespaces, a cleared task-command\n   156\tenvironment, a bounded wall clock, bounded captured output, per-run process-group teardown, and read-only runtime\n   157\tbinds. Black-box qualification keeps oracle expectations outside candidate namespaces. Legacy hidden commands share a\n   158\tprocess with candidate code and are rejected during qualification preflight. The hardened legacy path below is only for\n   159\tdevelopment and pilot scoring; it is not qualification evidence. Aggregate CPU, memory, process-count and\n   160\twritable-storage limits come from the resource contract below, not from request/token ceilings. Appliance-specific\n   161\tBubblewrap enforcement is a separate, unverified condition.\n   162\t\n   163\t### Hardened legacy hidden tests (development and pilot only)\n   164\t\n   165\tLegacy tasks use `hidden: { dir, command }` (for example `pytest` or `go test` over copied test files) and run candidate\n   166\tcode in the same process as the oracle. They do not meet the qualification boundary, even when hardened. Qualification\n   167\tpreflight accepts only the isolated `blackbox-v1` protocol. Any legacy task in a qualification corpus must be replaced\n   168\tor converted by the authorized custodian, with custody checks repeated and a new immutable manifest identity recorded.\n   169\tDevelopment configs may opt into the hardened diagnostic path with `\"hardenedChecks\": true`.\n   170\t\n   171\t1. The agent's workspace is never tested, and nothing runs git or any other program against it after the agent ran.\n   172\t   An agent-written `.git/config` or `.gitattributes` would otherwise run commands as the harness user. The baseline\n   173\t   tree hash is taken before the agent starts. The harness then stages the task again (the same pinned commit or\n   174\t   fixture, the same setup commands) into a fresh workspace, and checks that its tree equals that baseline.\n   175\t2. The agent's changes are found by walking both trees and comparing bytes and executable bits\n   176\t   (`eval/hardened_check.mjs`). Only an allowlist crosses over:\n   177\t   - Only **source files** (`.py`, the `.js`/`.ts` family, `.go`, `.rs`) and the manifests `package.json`,\n   178\t     `pyproject.toml`, `Cargo.toml`, `go.mod` and `go.sum` may be added, changed or deleted. Every other file type,\n   179\t     including test data, keeps its baseline content.\n   180\t   - A new Python file may only join a regular package that already exists, meaning a directory with `__init__.py` in\n   181\t     the baseline. It may not take a name that would shadow the test runner or the standard library\n   182\t     (`pytest.py`, `json.py`, …). Namespace packages without `__init__.py` cannot gain new modules; that is a\n   183\t     fairness cost.\n   184\t   - **Always dropped:**\n   185\t     - VCS metadata and dependency trees or caches: `.git`, `node_modules`, `.venv`, `site-packages`,\n   186\t       `__pycache__`, `vendor`, `target`, `.yarn`, `.cargo`, `.github`;\n   187\t     - test files and directories;\n   188\t     - test data: `testdata/`, `fixtures/`, `__snapshots__/`, `snapshots/`, `golden/`, mocks, `*.golden`, `*.snap`;\n   189\t     - test-runner configuration and setup files;\n   190\t     - interpreter and package-manager hooks: `sitecustomize.py`, `.pth`, `.npmrc`, `.yarnrc*`, `.pnpmfile.cjs`,\n   191\t       `go.work`, `rust-toolchain*`, `build.rs`;\n   192\t     - `Makefile`, `.env*`, `.gitattributes` and `.gitignore`;\n   193\t     - symlinks, any path that passes through a symlink in the fresh tree, and files over 2 MiB.\n   194\t\n   195\t     Dependency trees therefore always come from setup, never from the agent.\n   196\t   - **Manifests** may change only plain dependency or metadata entries. Any other change refuses both checks, and so\n   197\t     does a deleted manifest or a new one (a new `go.mod` or `package.json` moves module or root boundaries). In TOML,\n   198\t     every line starting with `[` is a section boundary, keyed by its exact text. An unrecognised header, a `[[…]]`\n   199\t     table, or a header with a comment therefore cannot be folded into an allowed section:\n   200\t     - `package.json`: anything but the dependency maps and metadata, or a `file:`/`git:`/path dependency;\n   201\t     - `pyproject.toml`: anything outside `[project]`;\n   202\t     - `Cargo.toml`: anything outside `[dependencies]`/`[dependencies.<name>]`, or a `path`, `git` or `registry`\n   203\t       source. Adding `[dev-dependencies]` or `[build-dependencies]` is refused, which costs legitimate test-only\n   204\t       dependency additions;\n   205\t     - `go.mod`: anything outside its `require` lines and blocks, including entries inside an existing `replace (…)`.\n   206\t3. Anything the agent left at the oracle's path is removed. The hidden command then runs with the hidden directory\n   207\t   bind-mounted **read-only**. The regression\n   208\t   command runs in a separate byte-identical copy, so candidate code executed by one check cannot prepare the other.\n   209\t4. In development/pilot runs, hidden-test output is kept only as a status, byte count and SHA-256, so diagnostic\n   210\t   records do not disclose the tests. Each run record carries\n   211\t   `hardened: { baseline, applied, discarded, configChanges }`.\n   212\t\n   213\tThis legacy hardening path supports development and pilot scoring only. Candidate source still executes in the same\n   214\truntime as its legacy validator, so it can inspect or alter validator state; file hiding and a fresh workspace do not\n   215\tprovide independent qualification. `qualification: true` rejects every legacy hidden command before any candidate\n   216\tstarts. Do not relabel legacy evidence as qualification evidence.\n   217\t\n   218\tThe legacy output checks below are diagnostic safeguards for development scoring, not acceptance evidence. Qualification\n   219\tkeeps black-box expected values in the evaluator process and runs candidate code in a separate Bubblewrap process.\n   220\t- Fairness cost: a real source directory named `vendor/` or `target/` is skipped like a dependency tree, so the\n# Nexus Code v1.0.0: audit and planning package\n\nThis package is the evidence-based path from `v0.1.0-alpha.2` (audited at `db1ca55`, 2026-10-14) to v1.0.0.\n\n**Verdict: v1.0.0 readiness is BLOCKED.** Candidate source revision `6151a231e9179c109cfbe8cff87e0a4148587b6a` passed an isolated `make build` and full `make test` run on 2026-10-16 (Rust 281/281; CLI 1087/1087; zero skipped). This is source-suite evidence, not RC qualification. Held-out task quality, T3/T4 qualification, standalone/provider qualification, the manual terminal matrix, package/install matrix on the RC, and independent RC acceptance remain incomplete. See [the current readiness reconciliation](READINESS.md) for exact open requirements and gates. The contest entry remains a separate track; judging has concluded and no contest freeze applies.\n\n## How to read it\n\n| If you want to… | Read |\n|---|---|\n| Understand what Nexus is today, what was examined, and what is wrong | [AUDIT.md](AUDIT.md) |\n| See current release readiness and remaining actions | [READINESS.md](READINESS.md) |\n| See every requirement (historical and new), its status, blocker class, and how it is accepted | [REQUIREMENTS.md](REQUIREMENTS.md) |\n| Know the order of work, the effort, and the critical path | [ROADMAP.md](ROADMAP.md) |\n| Implement a workstream | Its spec in [specs/](specs/), then its plan in [plans/](plans/) |\n| Qualify a release candidate | [ACCEPTANCE.md](ACCEPTANCE.md) |\n| Check what was actually run and what it proves | [EVIDENCE.md](EVIDENCE.md) and [evidence/](evidence/) |\n| See the decisions taken (all resolved under delegated owner authority) and the remaining owner actions | [DECISIONS.md](DECISIONS.md) |\n| Run several agents on this plan at once without collisions | [CONCURRENCY.md](CONCURRENCY.md) (also referenced from the root `AGENTS.md`) |\n\n## Workstreams\n\n| # | Workstream | Spec | Plan | Milestone | Blockers |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 01 | Safe execution defaults and approval integrity | [SPEC-01](specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md) | [PLAN-01](plans/PLAN-01-safe-execution-defaults.md) | M1 | SEC-101, 102, 103, 119 |\n| 02 | Process ownership, cancellation, resource bounds | [SPEC-02](specs/SPEC-02-process-ownership-and-bounds.md) | [PLAN-02](plans/PLAN-02-process-ownership-and-bounds.md) | M1 | AGT-109, SEC-106 |\n| 03 | Untrusted workspace inputs and egress boundary | [SPEC-03](specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md) | [PLAN-03](plans/PLAN-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md) | M1 | SEC-110, 111, 115, 116 |\n| 04 | Continuity, resume, durable work units | [SPEC-04](specs/SPEC-04-continuity-and-durable-work.md) | [PLAN-04](plans/PLAN-04-continuity-and-durable-work.md) | M3 | AGT-101, 102 |\n| 05 | Standalone local inference and provider robustness | [SPEC-05](specs/SPEC-05-standalone-local-inference.md) | [PLAN-05](plans/PLAN-05-standalone-local-inference.md) | M3/M5 | PRV-101, 102, 106 |\n| 06 | Packaged product parity, launcher, versioning, doctor | [SPEC-06](specs/SPEC-06-packaged-product-parity.md) | [PLAN-06](plans/PLAN-06-packaged-product-parity.md) | M0/M2 | REL-101, 102, 103 |\n| 07 | Supply chain, compliance, release engineering | [SPEC-07](specs/SPEC-07-supply-chain-and-release-engineering.md) | [PLAN-07](plans/PLAN-07-supply-chain-and-release-engineering.md) | M2 | REL-107 |\n| 08 | Persistence hygiene | [SPEC-08](specs/SPEC-08-persistence-hygiene.md) | [PLAN-08](plans/PLAN-08-persistence-hygiene.md) | M0/M4 | (prerequisite: PER-103) |\n| 09 | Requirements governance and documentation truth | [SPEC-09](specs/SPEC-09-requirements-governance-and-docs.md) | [PLAN-09](plans/PLAN-09-requirements-governance-and-docs.md) | M0/M6 | OPS-101, 103 |\n| 10 | v1 qualification and evaluation | [SPEC-10](specs/SPEC-10-v1-qualification-and-evaluation.md) | [PLAN-10](plans/PLAN-10-v1-qualification-and-evaluation.md) | M3/M5 | QUAL-101 to 107 |\n| 11 | Verification and tool-call integrity | [SPEC-11](specs/SPEC-11-verification-and-tool-call-integrity.md) | [PLAN-11](plans/PLAN-11-verification-and-tool-call-integrity.md) | M1/M3 | AGT-112, 113, 114 |\n| 12 | In-place CLI and TUI upgrade | [SPEC-12](specs/SPEC-12-cli-and-tui-upgrade.md) | [PLAN-12](plans/PLAN-12-cli-and-tui-upgrade.md) | M2 | REL-115 |\n| C1 | Contest (separate product; post-contest maintenance; no freeze applies) | [SPEC-C1](specs/SPEC-C1-contest-submission-track.md) | [PLAN-C1](plans/PLAN-C1-contest-submission-track.md) | post-contest | — (not a client gate) |\n\n\n## How to execute\n\n0. If more than one agent (or person) will implement in parallel, read [CONCURRENCY.md](CONCURRENCY.md) first. It covers slice claims, worktrees, hot files, the gateway lock and merge order.\n1. **Start with M0** (ROADMAP). Merge PLAN-08 PR-8a first, so that test runs stop writing into the real `~/.nexus`. Then land PLAN-06 PR-6a (version sync) and PLAN-09 PR-9a (ledger checker, historical banners, cheap doc fixes).\n2. **M1 is the critical path.** Work through PLAN-01, PLAN-02, PLAN-03 and PLAN-11 PR-11a/b in the PR slices listed. Each plan names its safe change boundary, code locations, tests and rollback.\n3. **For every PR:**\n   - update the matching REQUIREMENTS rows (status, test reference, commit);\n   - keep `scripts/check_requirements.mjs` green;\n   - run `make test` on a snapshot copy, never while editing (tasks/lessons.md).\n4. M2 and M4 run in parallel with M1/M3. Held-out task authoring (agent-authored, as authorised) and the standalone proxy start during M1.\n5. Cut `v1.0.0-rc.1` only when G0–G4 pass. Then run ACCEPTANCE §4–§8 against the **installed** RC.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- **Requirement IDs.** `REQ-<AREA>-0xx` marks a commitment carried over from history. `REQ-<AREA>-1xx` marks a new v1 requirement.\n- **Finding IDs.** These are owned by the scoped evidence files: AGT, VER, PRV, SEC, PER, TUI, REL, DX, MNT, HIST, EVL, CON, PERF, DOC.\n- **Acceptance IDs.** `ACC-<AREA>-NN`. Each is defined in its spec, or in ACCEPTANCE §3.1.\n- **Evidence labels.** SRC, TEST, RT, MEAS, DOC, HIST, INF, UNV (REQUIREMENTS §1).\n- **Status ladder.** open → partial → implemented → tested → visually-accepted → product-qualified. Independent acceptance is recorded separately.\n\nThis package supersedes the Issue #12 matrices and the PHASE and WP documents as the source of current requirements. Those documents remain as historical record.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":52471}},"started_at_ms":1792853763021,"completed_at_ms":1792853763022}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-24T14:56:04.240Z","ordinal":294259,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a16e69-6890-7402-8dfb-0564fda7a41f","call_id":"call_32d8dc8d90e34d7281769ae3eafb281c","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 7881)\nTotal output lines: 361\n\n   110\t| ACC-TUI-STEER | PTY: submit a task; while a tool runs, type steering text and press Enter; also queue a steer before a cancel | Steering is applied at the next step boundary. Unapplied steering is returned to the composer. Nothing is dropped silently (existing `sidecar_steering_diff` plus PTY s6). |\n   111\t| ACC-TUI-A11Y | PTY with `NO_COLOR=1` and with `TERM=dumb` at 40×14 and 100×30 | Zero colour SGR codes. Every state is distinguishable by glyph and word. Hints are complete at 40 columns (TUI-10). |\n   112\t| ACC-SEC-JAIL | `core/tests` plus harness-core calls with `..`, absolute, symlinked-file, symlinked-dir and symlinked-parent payloads | Every one is refused (C §4 R1 set). |\n   113\t| ACC-SEC-B6 | Re-run `docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/repro/zz_trust_repro.test.ts`. Seeded-secret scan of `~/.nexus` and `.nexus` after a scripted turn. | Zero plaintext seeded secrets in durable stores or outbound bodies. |\n   114\t| ACC-PRV-LIVE | `nexus providers verify --provider <p> -m <pinned model> -j` for each provider listed as supported (DEC-06) | `passed: true`. Receipts archived. |\n   115\t| ACC-PRV-STANDALONE | §4.2 on the installed RC through the approved stripped proxy, with gateway credentials absent from the child environment | Every required profile completes through the proxy and the absent-gateway boundary holds; receipts archived. |\n   116\t| ACC-PLAT | E2 matrix, §7 | All rows pass. |\n   117\t| ACC-QUAL-T2 | §6.1 + §6.4 | Thresholds met. |\n   118\t| ACC-QUAL-T3 | §6.3 + §6.4 | Thresholds met. |\n   119\t| ACC-QUAL-T4 | §6.2 + §6.4 | Thresholds met. |\n   120\t| ACC-QUAL-SMOKE | `make test` includes the T1 self-test and the T4 smoke | Present and passing. |\n   121\t| ACC-TUI-MANUAL | §5.3 on the installed RC across five required terminal environments and three required sizes | Every cell passes; screenshots and per-cell verdicts are archived. |\n   122\t\n   123\t## 4. Standalone and provider qualification (REQ-PRV-106, REQ-PRV-111, REQ-QUAL-107)\n   124\t\n   125\t### 4.1 Clean-HOME procedure\n   126\t\n   127\t1. `export HOME=$(mktemp -d)`.\n   128\t2. Install the RC with the documented command.\n   129\t3. Create `~/.config/nexus/config.env` containing only:\n   130\t   - `LOCAL_LLM_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:<proxy>/v1`\n   131\t   - `LOCAL_LLM_MODEL=<id as listed by the proxy>`\n   132\t   - `LOCAL_LLM_API_KEY=REDACTED\n   133\t\n   134\t   No gateway token file, no homelab paths, no `NODE_*` overrides.\n   135\t4. Start `node eval/standalone/plain_proxy.mjs --upstream <gateway> --token-file <operator token> [--emulate <kind>]`. The gateway token stays with the proxy and never reaches the Nexus HOME.\n   136\t5. Run `nexus doctor --json` and archive it. It must report the server kind and context source with no `gateway` capability.\n   137\t\n   138\t### 4.2 Standalone matrix\n   139\t\n   140\t| Profile | What Nexus sees | Runs | Pass condition |\n   141\t|---|---|---|---|\n   142\t| P-plain | Bare OpenAI-compatible `/models` and chat. No tokenize, no capacity headers. | Standalone T2 subset (≥ 10 held-out tasks) × 3 runs; T4 classes 1, 3, 4, 7 × 3 | T2 correctness ≥ (gateway-direct T2 correctness on the same tasks − 10 pts). False VERIFIED = 0. T4 truthful = 100%. Context source reported as `client_default` or an operator override, with no error. |\n   143\t| P-llamacpp | Emulated llama.cpp `/props` and overflow body | Same tasks × 1 run; plus the forced-overflow case (context limit set to 8k) | Context discovered from `/props`. Overflow gives one compact-and-retry, then success or an actionable error. |\n   144\t| P-vllm | Emulated `max_model_len` and vLLM overflow body | Same as P-llamacpp | Same, with the context source `max_model_len` |\n   145\t| P-ollama | Emulated `/api/show` and Ollama overflow body | Same as P-llamacpp | Same, with the context source `ollama_show` |\n   146\t| Conformance | CI fixtures for llama.cpp, vLLM, LM Studio and Ollama shapes | CI | All pass (ACC-INF-01, -02, -05) |\n   147\t\n   148\tACC-INF-10 passes only when every row passes. The release notes state the scope in the form required by DEC-16.\n   149\t\n   150\t### 4.3 Provider matrix (E5)\n   151\t\n   152\t| Provider | Tier (DEC-06) | Required for v1.0.0 |\n   153\t|---|---|---|\n   154\t| OpenRouter, pinned model | Supported | `providers verify` PASS, plus one live small task (a §6-style bug fix) reaching a correct outcome |\n   155\t| ChatGPT sign-in | Experimental | Verify PASS recorded (already PASS at db1ca55; repeat on the RC) |\n   156\t| OpenAI API key, Anthropic | Experimental unless keys are added | If promoted: verify PASS plus one live task. Otherwise NOT RUN, and they are labelled experimental. |\n   157\t| Copilot | Experimental | Not required |\n   158\t\n   159\t## 5. Terminal acceptance (REQ-QUAL-108)\n   160\t\n   161\t### 5.1 CI PTY suites (E1)\n   162\t\n   163\tThese must pass:\n   164\t\n   165\t- `tui_approval_input_e2e`\n   166\t- `tui_diff_e2e`\n   167\t- `tui_maturity_e2e`\n   168\t- `tui_model_picker_e2e`\n   169\t- `tui_multiline_e2e`\n   170\t- `tui_surface_e2e`\n   171\t- new: `repl_approval_e2e`\n   172\t- new: `process_lifecycle_e2e`\n   173\t- new: `startup_unreachable_e2e`\n   174\t- new: the package PTY smoke\n   175\t\n   176\t### 5.2 Scripted PTY scenarios against the installed RC (E3, mock gateway)\n   177\t\n   178\tThese are ports of `evidence/D_pty/`.\n   179\t\n   180\t| Scenario | Pass condition |\n   181\t|---|---|\n   182\t| s1 startup, unreachable endpoint | The endpoint is named, with a fix hint. The turn ends with guidance in ≤ 2.5 min. |\n   183\t| s2 approval under continuous typing, paste and burst, at 40×14 | No decision is made by stray input. A deliberate key decides. |\n   184\t| s3 completion | The outcome is shown in words: VERIFIED, REGRESSION_FREE or UNVERIFIED. |\n   185\t| s4–s6 cancel and steer | Cancel is acknowledged within 1 frame and idle within 5 s. Steering is applied. |\n   186\t| s7 resume | The prior conversation is shown. |\n   187\t| s8–s9 cancel or quit during a tool | Zero surviving processes. |\n   188\t| r1a / r1d REPL approvals | DENIED for a lone Enter and for a typed-ahead `y`. |\n   189\t\n   190\t### 5.3 Manual real-terminal matrix (E3)\n     1\t# Evaluation harness (qualification assessment §9)\n     2\t\n     3\t`run.mjs` runs tasks against one agent configuration and appends a JSON line per run; `score.mjs` reports the scores\n     4\tseparately (never combined), per task and configuration, with 95 % Wilson intervals.\n     5\t\n     6\t`docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md` is a historical checkpoint at source revision `6151a23`; use this file and the current\n     7\trunner source for evaluator behavior added after that revision. Neither document represents completed M5 qualification.\n     8\t\n     9\t```bash\n    10\tnode eval/run.mjs --config eval/configs/gateway-engineering-deep.json --out eval/out/<label> [--only id,id] [--runs N]\n    11\tnode eval/score.mjs eval/out/<label>/results.jsonl          # markdown table; --json for machine output\n    12\tnode eval/score.mjs --pairs \"$NEXUS_RESULTS\" \"$GOOSE_RESULTS\" \"$OPENCODE_RESULTS\"\n    13\t```\n    14\t\n    15\tEach run uses a frozen copy of the task tree, a fresh workspace and `HOME`, and a unique output directory. The agent\n    16\treceives only its workspace, isolated home, selected read-only runtime files, and a run-scoped token for a local proxy;\n    17\tthe real gateway credential stays in the harness process. The candidate runs in Bubblewrap user, PID and network\n    18\tnamespaces, with nested user namespaces disabled. Legacy development tasks may still use `hidden: { dir, into, command }`;\n    19\tthose commands execute against the candidate workspace after the agent exits and are **not safe for qualification**, because\n    20\tvalidator code can inspect or modify files in that workspace. Qualification requires `hidden: { protocol: \"blackbox-v1\",\n    21\tcasesFile, runtime, argv, timeoutMs, totalTimeoutMs, maxOutputBytes }`. Its private JSON cases contain only `input` and\n    22\t`expected`; the host evaluator keeps expected values in its process and launches the candidate runtime separately for each\n    23\tinput using a fresh top-level Bubblewrap boundary. The candidate receives one input on stdin and returns one JSON value on\n    24\tstdout. Its workspace is read-only; private oracle files are not mounted. Candidate output and diagnostics are discarded,\n    25\tand result logs contain only case counts and failed indexes. Supported runtimes are explicitly allowlisted (`node`,\n    26\t`python3`); the only argument is a validated relative candidate entrypoint (no runtime flags, eval expressions, or extra\n    27\targuments), and per-case/aggregate time and output bounds are enforced. Oracle files must be outside the copied fixture.\n    28\tAbsolute paths, traversal, symlinks and unsupported fixture entries are rejected. `manifest.json` records the harness source-file hashes, model fingerprint, corpus/task\n    29\thashes when qualification is configured, executable version, budgets, host and Node version. Each result records the\n…3381 tokens truncated…ore`;\n   193\t     - symlinks, any path that passes through a symlink in the fresh tree, and files over 2 MiB.\n   194\t\n   195\t     Dependency trees therefore always come from setup, never from the agent.\n   196\t   - **Manifests** may change only plain dependency or metadata entries. Any other change refuses both checks, and so\n   197\t     does a deleted manifest or a new one (a new `go.mod` or `package.json` moves module or root boundaries). In TOML,\n   198\t     every line starting with `[` is a section boundary, keyed by its exact text. An unrecognised header, a `[[…]]`\n   199\t     table, or a header with a comment therefore cannot be folded into an allowed section:\n   200\t     - `package.json`: anything but the dependency maps and metadata, or a `file:`/`git:`/path dependency;\n   201\t     - `pyproject.toml`: anything outside `[project]`;\n   202\t     - `Cargo.toml`: anything outside `[dependencies]`/`[dependencies.<name>]`, or a `path`, `git` or `registry`\n   203\t       source. Adding `[dev-dependencies]` or `[build-dependencies]` is refused, which costs legitimate test-only\n   204\t       dependency additions;\n   205\t     - `go.mod`: anything outside its `require` lines and blocks, including entries inside an existing `replace (…)`.\n   206\t3. Anything the agent left at the oracle's path is removed. The hidden command then runs with the hidden directory\n   207\t   bind-mounted **read-only**. The regression\n   208\t   command runs in a separate byte-identical copy, so candidate code executed by one check cannot prepare the other.\n   209\t4. In development/pilot runs, hidden-test output is kept only as a status, byte count and SHA-256, so diagnostic\n   210\t   records do not disclose the tests. Each run record carries\n   211\t   `hardened: { baseline, applied, discarded, configChanges }`.\n   212\t\n   213\tThis legacy hardening path supports development and pilot scoring only. Candidate source still executes in the same\n   214\truntime as its legacy validator, so it can inspect or alter validator state; file hiding and a fresh workspace do not\n   215\tprovide independent qualification. `qualification: true` rejects every legacy hidden command before any candidate\n   216\tstarts. Do not relabel legacy evidence as qualification evidence.\n   217\t\n   218\tThe legacy output checks below are diagnostic safeguards for development scoring, not acceptance evidence. Qualification\n   219\tkeeps black-box expected values in the evaluator process and runs candidate code in a separate Bubblewrap process.\n   220\t- Fairness cost: a real source directory named `vendor/` or `target/` is skipped like a dependency tree, so the\n# Nexus Code v1.0.0: audit and planning package\n\nThis package is the evidence-based path from `v0.1.0-alpha.2` (audited at `db1ca55`, 2026-10-14) to v1.0.0.\n\n**Verdict: v1.0.0 readiness is BLOCKED.** Candidate source revision `6151a231e9179c109cfbe8cff87e0a4148587b6a` passed an isolated `make build` and full `make test` run on 2026-10-16 (Rust 281/281; CLI 1087/1087; zero skipped). This is source-suite evidence, not RC qualification. Held-out task quality, T3/T4 qualification, standalone/provider qualification, the manual terminal matrix, package/install matrix on the RC, and independent RC acceptance remain incomplete. See [the current readiness reconciliation](READINESS.md) for exact open requirements and gates. The contest entry remains a separate track; judging has concluded and no contest freeze applies.\n\n## How to read it\n\n| If you want to… | Read |\n|---|---|\n| Understand what Nexus is today, what was examined, and what is wrong | [AUDIT.md](AUDIT.md) |\n| See current release readiness and remaining actions | [READINESS.md](READINESS.md) |\n| See every requirement (historical and new), its status, blocker class, and how it is accepted | [REQUIREMENTS.md](REQUIREMENTS.md) |\n| Know the order of work, the effort, and the critical path | [ROADMAP.md](ROADMAP.md) |\n| Implement a workstream | Its spec in [specs/](specs/), then its plan in [plans/](plans/) |\n| Qualify a release candidate | [ACCEPTANCE.md](ACCEPTANCE.md) |\n| Check what was actually run and what it proves | [EVIDENCE.md](EVIDENCE.md) and [evidence/](evidence/) |\n| See the decisions taken (all resolved under delegated owner authority) and the remaining owner actions | [DECISIONS.md](DECISIONS.md) |\n| Run several agents on this plan at once without collisions | [CONCURRENCY.md](CONCURRENCY.md) (also referenced from the root `AGENTS.md`) |\n\n## Workstreams\n\n| # | Workstream | Spec | Plan | Milestone | Blockers |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 01 | Safe execution defaults and approval integrity | [SPEC-01](specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md) | [PLAN-01](plans/PLAN-01-safe-execution-defaults.md) | M1 | SEC-101, 102, 103, 119 |\n| 02 | Process ownership, cancellation, resource bounds | [SPEC-02](specs/SPEC-02-process-ownership-and-bounds.md) | [PLAN-02](plans/PLAN-02-process-ownership-and-bounds.md) | M1 | AGT-109, SEC-106 |\n| 03 | Untrusted workspace inputs and egress boundary | [SPEC-03](specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md) | [PLAN-03](plans/PLAN-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md) | M1 | SEC-110, 111, 115, 116 |\n| 04 | Continuity, resume, durable work units | [SPEC-04](specs/SPEC-04-continuity-and-durable-work.md) | [PLAN-04](plans/PLAN-04-continuity-and-durable-work.md) | M3 | AGT-101, 102 |\n| 05 | Standalone local inference and provider robustness | [SPEC-05](specs/SPEC-05-standalone-local-inference.md) | [PLAN-05](plans/PLAN-05-standalone-local-inference.md) | M3/M5 | PRV-101, 102, 106 |\n| 06 | Packaged product parity, launcher, versioning, doctor | [SPEC-06](specs/SPEC-06-packaged-product-parity.md) | [PLAN-06](plans/PLAN-06-packaged-product-parity.md) | M0/M2 | REL-101, 102, 103 |\n| 07 | Supply chain, compliance, release engineering | [SPEC-07](specs/SPEC-07-supply-chain-and-release-engineering.md) | [PLAN-07](plans/PLAN-07-supply-chain-and-release-engineering.md) | M2 | REL-107 |\n| 08 | Persistence hygiene | [SPEC-08](specs/SPEC-08-persistence-hygiene.md) | [PLAN-08](plans/PLAN-08-persistence-hygiene.md) | M0/M4 | (prerequisite: PER-103) |\n| 09 | Requirements governance and documentation truth | [SPEC-09](specs/SPEC-09-requirements-governance-and-docs.md) | [PLAN-09](plans/PLAN-09-requirements-governance-and-docs.md) | M0/M6 | OPS-101, 103 |\n| 10 | v1 qualification and evaluation | [SPEC-10](specs/SPEC-10-v1-qualification-and-evaluation.md) | [PLAN-10](plans/PLAN-10-v1-qualification-and-evaluation.md) | M3/M5 | QUAL-101 to 107 |\n| 11 | Verification and tool-call integrity | [SPEC-11](specs/SPEC-11-verification-and-tool-call-integrity.md) | [PLAN-11](plans/PLAN-11-verification-and-tool-call-integrity.md) | M1/M3 | AGT-112, 113, 114 |\n| 12 | In-place CLI and TUI upgrade | [SPEC-12](specs/SPEC-12-cli-and-tui-upgrade.md) | [PLAN-12](plans/PLAN-12-cli-and-tui-upgrade.md) | M2 | REL-115 |\n| C1 | Contest (separate product; post-contest maintenance; no freeze applies) | [SPEC-C1](specs/SPEC-C1-contest-submission-track.md) | [PLAN-C1](plans/PLAN-C1-contest-submission-track.md) | post-contest | — (not a client gate) |\n\n\n## How to execute\n\n0. If more than one agent (or person) will implement in parallel, read [CONCURRENCY.md](CONCURRENCY.md) first. It covers slice claims, worktrees, hot files, the gateway lock and merge order.\n1. **Start with M0** (ROADMAP). Merge PLAN-08 PR-8a first, so that test runs stop writing into the real `~/.nexus`. Then land PLAN-06 PR-6a (version sync) and PLAN-09 PR-9a (ledger checker, historical banners, cheap doc fixes).\n2. **M1 is the critical path.** Work through PLAN-01, PLAN-02, PLAN-03 and PLAN-11 PR-11a/b in the PR slices listed. Each plan names its safe change boundary, code locations, tests and rollback.\n3. **For every PR:**\n   - update the matching REQUIREMENTS rows (status, test reference, commit);\n   - keep `scripts/check_requirements.mjs` green;\n   - run `make test` on a snapshot copy, never while editing (tasks/lessons.md).\n4. M2 and M4 run in parallel with M1/M3. Held-out task authoring (agent-authored, as authorised) and the standalone proxy start during M1.\n5. Cut `v1.0.0-rc.1` only when G0–G4 pass. Then run ACCEPTANCE §4–§8 against the **installed** RC.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- **Requirement IDs.** `REQ-<AREA>-0xx` marks a commitment carried over from history. `REQ-<AREA>-1xx` marks a new v1 requirement.\n- **Finding IDs.** These are owned by the scoped evidence files: AGT, VER, PRV, SEC, PER, TUI, REL, DX, MNT, HIST, EVL, CON, PERF, DOC.\n- **Acceptance IDs.** `ACC-<AREA>-NN`. Each is defined in its spec, or in ACCEPTANCE §3.1.\n- **Evidence labels.** SRC, TEST, RT, MEAS, DOC, HIST, INF, UNV (REQUIREMENTS §1).\n- **Status ladder.** open → partial → implemented → tested → visually-accepted → product-qualified. Independent acceptance is recorded separately.\n\nThis package supersedes the Issue #12 matrices and the PHASE and WP documents as the source of current requirements. Those documents remain as historical record.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a16e62-7365-74b3-a660-6c02335c1b7f","create_time":1792853764.2405794}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
