{"timestamp":"2026-10-24T14:58:05.059Z","ordinal":294356,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a16e62-7365-74b3-a660-6c02335c1b7f","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-fb886a3e-e655-4589-bdda-1312544d5a1e","process_id":"98325","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","git grep -n -e 'Sensitive content prevents durable persistence to checkpoint' -e 'sensitive content' -e 'durable persistence' origin/main -- cli/src core/src docs | head -100; git grep -n -e 'Sensitive content prevents durable persistence to checkpoint' v1.0.0-rc.4 -- cli/src core/src | head -40; gh pr list --state open --search 'checkpoint sensitive' --json number,title,headRefName,updatedAt,reviewDecision --limit 10"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"git grep -n -e 'Sensitive content prevents durable persistence to checkpoint' -e 'sensitive content' -e 'durable persistence' origin/main -- cli/src core/src docs | head -100; git grep -n -e 'Sensitive content prevents durable persistence to checkpoint' v1.0.0-rc.4 -- cli/src core/src | head -40; gh pr list --state open --search 'checkpoint sensitive' --json number,title,headRefName,updatedAt,reviewDecision --limit 10"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"origin/main:cli/src/core/app.ts:470:   * Checkpoints state to durable persistence.\norigin/main:cli/src/core/persistence_protection.ts:12:    super(`Sensitive content prevents durable persistence to ${destination}.`);\norigin/main:cli/src/repl.ts:190:    console.log(\"  3. Create the checkpoint anyway (may persist sensitive content)\");\norigin/main:cli/src/sidecar.ts:677:        message: \"Sensitive content blocked the checkpoint. Choose 1 read-only, 2 continue without a rollback checkpoint, 3 create a checkpoint anyway (may persist sensitive content), 4 cancel this turn, or 5 re-enter the instruction.\",\norigin/main:core/src/bin/nexus_tui/live_view.rs:2643:        feed(&mut state, &[r#\"{\"v\":1,\"type\":\"checkpoint.choice.request\",\"id\":\"choice-1\",\"turn_id\":\"t1\",\"message\":\"Sensitive content blocked the checkpoint. Choose 1 read-only, 2 continue without a rollback checkpoint, 3 create a checkpoint anyway (may persist sensitive content), 4 cancel this turn, or 5 re-enter the instruction.\"}\"#]);\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_ASSESSMENT.md:16:- `PERS-002`: durable persistence paths must not create uncontrolled unredacted copies of credentials or classified data.\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_MATRIX.md:91:| PERS-001 | 14: Persistence & Recovery | The architecture MUST formally distinguish between: (1) local volatile processing; (2) local durable persistence; (3) outbound disclosure; (4) publication/export; (5) retention; (6) deletion; and (7) recovery. | Security and authority policies MUST treat local durable storage as distinct from outbound network egress while preventing uncontrolled local leakage. | Phase 2 §18 | `cli/src/core/state.ts`; cli/test/state.test.ts, cli/test/system_journey.test.ts | DEMONSTRATED | None | Satisfied |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_MATRIX.md:93:| PERS-003 | 14: Persistence & Recovery | Sensitive-data protection mechanisms applied to durable persistence MUST NOT be destructive in a manner that renders authorized local recovery or legitimate engineering work impossible. | Where persistent state requires sensitive configuration, data MUST be stored in protected/encrypted local vaults rather than destructively scrambled. | Phase 2 §18 | `cli/src/core/state.ts`; cli/test/state.test.ts, cli/test/system_journey.test.ts | DEMONSTRATED | None | Satisfied |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_POST_WP05_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:106:| `PERS-003` | 14: Persistence & Recovery | WP-07 (INC-08) | Sensitive-data protection mechanisms applied to durable persistence MUST NOT be destruc... | `cli/src/core/state.ts` | cli/test/state.test.ts, cli/test/system_journey.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:103:| `PERS-003` | 14: Persistence & Recovery | WP-07 (INC-08) | Sensitive-data protection mechanisms applied to durable persistence MUST NOT be destruc... | `cli/src/core/state.ts` | cli/test/state.test.ts, cli/test/system_journey.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:460:| **PERS-001** | The architecture MUST formally distinguish between: (1) local volatile processing; (2) local durable persistence; (3) outbound disclosure; (4) publication/export; (5) retention; (6) deletion; and (7) recovery. | Security and authority policies MUST treat local durable storage as distinct from outbound network egress while preventing uncontrolled local leakage. |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:462:| **PERS-003** | Sensitive-data protection mechanisms applied to durable persistence MUST NOT be destructive in a manner that renders authorized local recovery or legitimate engineering work impossible. | Where persistent state requires sensitive configuration, data MUST be stored in protected/encrypted local vaults rather than destructively scrambled. |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE2_REQUIREMENTS_INDEPENDENT_REVIEW.md:130:- **Why It Matters:** `DISC-002` classifies local `session persistence files` and `flight logs` as outbound egress sinks. This directly contradicts `PERS-001`, which requires the system to formally distinguish local durable persistence from outbound disclosure. Local session persistence is not outbound egress. Egress protection mechanisms (`DISC-005`) apply irreversible redaction or encryption to outbound destinations. If local session files are treated as outbound egress, the runtime would be required to irreversibly redact or opaquely tokenize local session files before saving them to disk.\norigin/main:docs/PHASE2_REQUIREMENTS_INDEPENDENT_REVIEW.md:352:`PERS-001` establishes the correct lifecycle model: distinguishing local processing, durable persistence, outbound disclosure, retention, and deletion. `PERS-004` ensures resuming a session does not resurrect stale authority.\norigin/main:docs/PHASE4_IMPLEMENTATION_WORK_PACKAGES.md:176:  3. `WP-04` gates durable persistence (`WP-07`) and provenance (`WP-09`). Resolving `DEC-02` is required to bind validation receipts.\norigin/main:docs/WP07_INDEPENDENT_IMPLEMENTATION_REVIEW.md:59:| `PERS-001` | Session & task state recovery; distinct durable persistence vs volatile memory | `cli/src/core/state.ts` (`DurableStateManager`, `DurableTaskState`), `cli/src/session.ts` (`SessionData`) | **SATISFIED** | Round-trip milestone persistence; active plan phases; restart idempotence |\norigin/main:docs/releases/v1.0.0-rc.3.md:12:- Adds an interactive checkpoint decision when sensitive content cannot be persisted, allowing the user to choose read-only, continue without a rollback checkpoint, create a checkpoint after acknowledging the sensitivity warning, cancel, or revise the instruction. This behavior must be verified from the installed candidate; RC1 and RC2 do not contain it.\norigin/main:docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/repro/zz_trust_repro.test.ts:240:test(\"R11 secret formats: model egress and durable persistence (flight recorder on)\", async () => {\norigin/main:docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:9:**RC3, checkpoint choice, and Doctor:** RC3 remains the latest published prerelease, tagged at `1b9791f07d301c885fe48f04a796203583ab5a5c`; its archive digest and provenance are recorded in the snapshot below. The operator's `Sensitive content prevents durable persistence to checkpoint` failure occurred on immutable RC1, which predates the interactive choice flow. RC3 source contains the five choices: read-only, continue in the configured mode without a rollback checkpoint, create a checkpoint with the sensitivity risk acknowledged, cancel, or re-enter the instruction. Current RC3 source also scans saved-state metadata asynchronously in bounded batches, accepts schema-2 task `messageLog` records, and treats a configured model absent from `/models` as unverified rather than unavailable; a `--deep` inference probe can verify it. The interrupted-write temporary file reported by Doctor is a separate real warning and is not automatically repaired. These source checks and existing tests do not substitute for exercising the exact installed candidate on the operator's own state.\norigin/main:docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:49:**Checkpoint failure and remediation:** The operator reports every launch attempt on installed RC1 fails before the turn starts with `Sensitive content prevents durable persistence to checkpoint`. This is confirmed as an RC1 limitation: immutable RC1 source predates the checkpoint-choice flow. PR #153 integrates that flow in current main, offering read-only, continue without a rollback checkpoint, create the checkpoint despite sensitive content, cancel, or re-enter the instruction. This is source-integrated behavior only; the installed candidate must exercise the prompt and selected actions before acceptance. RC1 and RC2 remain unchanged and do not contain this behavior.\norigin/main:docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:65:**Checkpoint failure and remediation:** The operator reports that every attempt to start Nexus RC1 fails with `Sensitive content prevents durable persistence to checkpoint`. This is consistent with RC1's exact source predating PR #147's checkpoint-choice flow. PR #147 is integrated in current main and its exact-head CI and CodeQL passed; the interactive TUI now offers read-only, continue without a rollback checkpoint, create the checkpoint anyway with a sensitivity warning, cancel, or re-enter the instruction. RC1 and RC2 are immutable and do not contain this behavior. The next candidate must include the fix, and the installed candidate must be exercised to verify the choices before any acceptance claim.\n[]\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":636512870}},"started_at_ms":1792853884421,"completed_at_ms":1792853885058}}
