{"timestamp":"2026-10-24T16:48:12.326Z","ordinal":297221,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a16ec6-5068-7a43-a634-88dec514377c","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-997e6355-a300-4d9c-9279-d0dd95889a73","process_id":"81337","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","git ls-remote origin refs/heads/main refs/heads/v1/integration refs/tags/v1.0.0-rc.4 refs/tags/v1.0.0-rc.5 && git show-ref --heads --tags | rg 'rc4|rc5|main|integration|startup' | tail -40 && rg -n \"trust.*workspace|Trust this folder|startup_trust|continue untrusted|Load project instructions\" . -g '!**/node_modules/**' -g '!**/.git/**' -g '!docs/v1.0.0/evidence/**' --max-count 20"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"git ls-remote origin refs/heads/main refs/heads/v1/integration refs/tags/v1.0.0-rc.4 refs/tags/v1.0.0-rc.5 && git show-ref --heads --tags | rg 'rc4|rc5|main|integration|startup' | tail -40 && rg -n \"trust.*workspace|Trust this folder|startup_trust|continue untrusted|Load project instructions\" . -g '!**/node_modules/**' -g '!**/.git/**' -g '!docs/v1.0.0/evidence/**' --max-count 20"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"41b381ddac182b7f0c783b33df5f1355518f465b\trefs/heads/main\n7d6673f108316dda015b858c85b2abe20f3a4f78\trefs/heads/v1/integration\n79b2f4fab6b9584c4e4e3245a5359f009889b7e2\trefs/tags/v1.0.0-rc.4\nf9d2743decd2dd3a4816f3bd8b5ec7f00d7a8801 refs/heads/codex/pr127-vite-current-main\ne13924963a7ced8a2dadfb007204cf2a8a937ab6 refs/heads/codex/retire-contest-contract-main\ndb1ca558086014912b20dae8232af111ccb21d67 refs/heads/main\nb947ddb43fd3acd67046a553e00037c6a536cf9d refs/heads/v1/docs-dev-pinned-builder-current-main\n519398495b7c3a2e5915ed76eee0bb731feb0bcf refs/heads/v1/integration\n0027ccccb1fbe3bac98bf53a8dd7c1c4bec3e8d3 refs/heads/v1/plan-10-host-preflight-main-1f3c655\n41df6a74745187585bb129013effdd3b173d78ae refs/heads/v1/plan-c1-post-contest-maintenance\n132719cf3e8fac735427820c764a560e4fc77a97 refs/heads/v1/rc3-candidate-main\n43c3d884a028f286443f7e8fd6d7a5fd4c947c83 refs/heads/v1/rc4-notes-completeness\nf13f9ef979ccf7a324a7af236357e035dcd08007 refs/heads/v1/rc4-notes-security-updates\n0cff3c3c142c01b837d03f37006d76deae7ead1e refs/heads/v1/rc4-version-sync\n41b381ddac182b7f0c783b33df5f1355518f465b refs/heads/v1/rc5-version-sync\nbe2cd2f255414f554572c86baa884d9271ff5ea1 refs/heads/v1/readiness-rc4-refresh\nda64255de37f3bab8ccdc5fed48bbeedf1a70b88 refs/heads/v1/startup-workspace-trust\nb14b314fab6dbf57b3ad029f5b8848fdff72fabe refs/heads/v1/startup-workspace-trust-rebased\n./cli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts:11:import { isWorkspaceTrusted, listTrustedWorkspaces, setWorkspaceTrusted, untrustedWorkspaceExtensions } from \"../src/workspace_trust.js\";\n./cli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts:68:      const store = path.join(home, \".config\", \"nexus\", \"trusted_workspaces.json\");\n./cli/test/doctor.test.ts:259:      // Untrusted: the workspace's own configuration is still inspected, and reported as not loaded.\n./cli/test/doctor.test.ts:263:      assert.match(find(untrusted, \"mcp.config\")!.evidence.join(\"\\n\"), /\\.mcp\\.json: not loaded — this workspace is not trusted\\. Trust it with `nexus trust/);\n./cli/test/doctor.test.ts:265:      assert.match(find(untrusted, \"plugins.config\")!.evidence.join(\"\\n\"), /p\\.mjs: not loaded — this workspace is not trusted/);\n./nexus-session-session_20261023_134635_z1dnlfek.json:41:      \"independenceDetail\": \"The workspace-resolved verifier has no matching pre-change baseline. Its current output cannot establish verification; start a fresh task from an operator-reviewed trusted workspace baseline.\",\n./nexus-session-session_20261023_134635_z1dnlfek.json:161:      \"content\": \"You are Nexus Code, an autonomous AI software engineering agent running in a Linux shell environment.\\nYou solve coding problems, build features, fix bugs, and refactor codebases by iteratively exploring, reading, editing, and verifying files.\\n\\n### Operating Rules:\\n1. EXPLORE FIRST: Before editing files, always explore the workspace using `file_tree`, `list_dir`, `find_files`, `search_code`, or `read_file`. For large files, use `outline_file` first to inspect class/function signatures without loading the entire file into context.\\n2. HIGH-EFFICIENCY BATCHING: Prefer `batch_read` to read multiple files concurrently in a single turn, and `file_info` to inspect metadata without loading whole files.\\n3. PRECISE EDITS & PATCHES: When modifying existing files, use `edit_file` with unique snippets or `patch_file` with unified diffs.\\n4. PLANNING: Use `update_plan` only for work with three or more distinct phases (not for a fix and its test); update it alongside other tool calls.\\n5. VERIFICATION: After making code changes, use `run_command` with bash/zsh commands to run tests, builds, or linting (e.g. `cargo check`, `cargo test`, `npm test`, `pytest`).\\n6. SELF-CORRECTION: If a command or tool returns an error, analyze the error message, identify the root cause, and fix it.\\n7. AUTONOMOUS COMPLETION: Keep executing tools until the entire task is thoroughly resolved. When everything is verified and done, invoke `task_completed` with a short summary of what changed and how it was checked.\\n8. BOUNDED CONTEXT: Your context window is limited, and earlier tool output is compacted out of the conversation as you work; your own messages are what carry forward. After inspecting large output you will rely on later, state its key findings in a line or two (with file and symbol references) before moving on; do not restate small files or narrate routine steps. Re-read earlier output only when you need a specific detail you did not record or it may have changed.\\n\\n\\n\\nAlways explain your reasoning before or alongside your tool calls.\\n\\n### Shell Environment:\\n- Active Shell: bash (standard GNU/Linux shell environment)\\n- Workspace directory: /home/mike/nexus-code\\n- Test command (Nexus verifies with it): `make test`\\n\\n### Project Instructions (loaded from AGENTS.md):\\n# Project Guidelines & Agent Instructions\\n\\n## Commands\\n- Build: `make build` or `cargo build` or `npm run build`\\n- Test: `make test` or `cargo test` or `npm test`\\n- Lint: `cargo clippy` or `npm run lint`\\n\\n## Code Conventions\\n- Maintain documentation integrity and keep existing comments.\\n- Always run tests after making changes to verify correctness.\\n- Keep functions modular and clearly typed.\\n\\n## v1.0.0 work and concurrent agents\\n- The v1.0.0 plan is in `docs/v1.0.0/` (start at `docs/v1.0.0/README.md`). Implement one claimed PR slice at a time, within the safe change boundary in its plan.\\n- When more than one agent may be working, follow `docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md`. In short:\\n  - Claim the slice atomically under `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/`.\\n  - Use one worktree and branch per slice; never edit `~/nexus-code` directly.\\n  - Isolate `HOME` for every test or probe run.\\n  - Claim hot files before editing them.\\n  - Take the `GATEWAY` claim for any inference run.\\n  - Never use `pkill -f`.\\n  - Check `git log` for foreign commits before every commit.\\n  - Leave push, tag and publish to the owner.\\n\\n### Specialized Agent Skills Available:\\nYou have access to specialized skills. If a user task matches a skill's description, call the `activate_skill` tool with the skill's name to load its specialized workflows and guidelines into context.\\n\\n- **graphify** [source: /home/mike/.config/opencode/skills/graphify/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Use for any question about a codebase, its architecture, file relationships, or project content — especially when graphify-out/ exists, where the question should be treated as a graphify query first. Turns any input (code, docs, papers, images, videos) into a persistent knowledge graph with god nodes, community detection, and query/path/explain tools.\\n- **gstack** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/_gstack-command/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Router for the gstack skill suite. (gstack)\\n- **autoplan** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/autoplan/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Auto-review pipeline — reads the full CEO, design, eng, and DX review skills from disk and runs them sequentially with auto-decisions using 6 decision principles. (gstack)\\n- **autoresearch-v2** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/autoresearch-v2/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Autonomous skill improvement loop inspired by Karpathy's autoresearch. Analyzes any skill, generates evals, runs baseline, then iteratively improves through autonomous experiments. v2: streamlined setup, blind scoring, resume capability, explicit execution protocol. Trigger with '/autoresearch-v2' followed by a skill name or file path. Examples: '/autoresearch-v2 seo-content-optimizer', '/autoresearch-v2 path/to/SKILL.md'.\\n- **benchmark** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/benchmark/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Performance regression detection using the browse daemon. (gstack)\\n- **benchmark-models** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/benchmark-models/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Cross-model benchmark for gstack skills. (gstack)\\n- **boris** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/boris/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: |\\n- **browse** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/browse/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Fast headless browser for QA testing and site dogfooding. (gstack)\\n- **bubbletea-v2** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/bubbletea-v2/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: >\\n- **canary** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/canary/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Post-deploy canary monitoring. (gstack)\\n- **careful** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/careful/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Safety guardrails for destructive commands. (gstack)\\n- **codex** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/codex/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: OpenAI Codex CLI wrapper — three modes. (gstack)\\n- **open-gstack-browser** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/connect-chrome/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Launch GStack Browser — AI-controlled Chromium with the sidebar extension baked in.\\n- **context-restore** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/context-restore/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Restore working context saved earlier by /context-save. (gstack)\\n- **context-save** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/context-save/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Save working context. (gstack)\\n- **cso** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/cso/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Chief Security Officer mode. (gstack)\\n- **design-consultation** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/design-consultation/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Design consultation: understands your product, researches the landscape, proposes a complete design system (aesthetic, typography, color, layout, spacing, motion), and generates font+color preview... (gstack)\\n- **design-html** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/design-html/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Design finalization: generates production-quality Pretext-native HTML/CSS. (gstack)\\n- **design-review** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/design-review/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Designer's eye QA: finds visual inconsistency, spacing issues, hierarchy problems, AI slop patterns, and slow interactions — then fixes them. (gstack)\\n- **design-shotgun** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/design-shotgun/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Design shotgun: generate multiple AI design variants, open a comparison board, collect structured feedback, and iterate. (gstack)\\n- **devex-review** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/devex-review/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Live developer experience audit. (gstack)\\n- **diagram** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/diagram/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Turn an English description (or mermaid source) into a diagram triplet: the source, an editable .excalidraw file you can open (gstack)\\n- **document-generate** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/document-generate/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Generate missing documentation from scratch for a feature, module, or entire project. (gstack)\\n- **document-release** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/document-release/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Post-ship documentation update. (gstack)\\n- **excalidraw-diagram** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/excalidraw-diagram/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Use when someone asks to sketch a system, map out a workflow, or build an editable visual diagram. Default for all diagram requests.\\n- **freeze** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/freeze/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Restrict file edits to a specific directory for the session. (gstack)\\n- **gitlab-operations** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/gitlab-operations/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: >\\n- **glab** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/glab/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: GitLab CLI (glab) for merge requests, issues, and CI/CD pipelines. Use when working with GitLab repositories for MR creation/review, issue management, pipeline debugging, or any GitLab API operations. Triggers on GitLab URLs, mentions of \\\"merge request\\\" or \\\"MR\\\" (not \\\"PR\\\"), gitlab.com, or glab commands.\\n- **gstack-upgrade** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/gstack-upgrade/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Upgrade gstack to the latest version.\\n- **guard** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/guard/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Full safety mode: destructive command warnings + directory-scoped edits. (gstack)\\n- **health** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/health/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Code quality dashboard. (gstack)\\n- **impeccable** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/impeccable/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Use when the user wants to design, redesign, shape, critique, audit, polish, clarify, distill, harden, optimize, adapt, animate, colorize, extract, or otherwise improve a frontend interface. Covers websites, landing pages, dashboards, product UI, app shells, components, forms, settings, onboarding, and empty states. Handles UX review, visual hierarchy, information architecture, cognitive load, accessibility, performance, responsive behavior, theming, anti-patterns, typography, fonts, spacing, layout, alignment, color, motion, micro-interactions, UX copy, error states, edge cases, i18n, and reusable design systems or tokens. Also use for bland designs that need to become bolder or more delightful, loud designs that should become quieter, live browser iteration on UI elements, or ambitious visual effects that should feel technically extraordinary. Not for backend-only or non-UI tasks.\\n- **investigate** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/investigate/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Systematic debugging with root cause investigation. (gstack)\\n- **ios-clean** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/ios-clean/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Remove the DebugBridge SPM package and all #if DEBUG wiring from an iOS app. (gstack)\\n- **ios-design-review** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/ios-design-review/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Visual design audit for iOS apps on real hardware. (gstack)\\n- **ios-fix** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/ios-fix/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Autonomous iOS bug fixer. (gstack)\\n- **ios-qa** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/ios-qa/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Live-device iOS QA for SwiftUI apps. (gstack)\\n- **ios-sync** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/ios-sync/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Regenerate the iOS debug bridge against the latest upstream gstack templates. (gstack)\\n- **land-and-deploy** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/land-and-deploy/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Land and deploy workflow. (gstack)\\n- **landing-report** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/landing-report/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Read-only queue dashboard for workspace-aware ship. (gstack)\\n- **learn** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/learn/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Manage project learnings.\\n- **loki-mode** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/loki-mode/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Version 2.35.0 | PRD to Production | Zero Human Intervention > Research-enhanced: OpenAI SDK, DeepMind, Anthropic, AWS Bedrock, Agent SDK, HN Production (2025)\\n- **make-pdf** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/make-pdf/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Turn any markdown file into a publication-quality PDF. (gstack)\\n- **office-hours** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/office-hours/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: YC Office Hours — two modes. (gstack)\\n- **pair-agent** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/pair-agent/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Pair a remote AI agent with your browser. (gstack)\\n- **cms-migration** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/payload-skills/skills/cms-migration/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Use when user wants to migrate content from another CMS (WordPress, Contentful, Strapi, Sanity, Webflow, etc.) to Payload CMS\\n- **payload** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/payload-skills/skills/payload/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Use when working with Payload projects (payload.config.ts, collections, fields, hooks, access control, Payload API). Use when debugging validation errors, security issues, relationship queries, transactions, or hook behavior.\\n- **plan-ceo-review** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/plan-ceo-review/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: CEO/founder-mode plan review. (gstack)\\n- **plan-design-review** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/plan-design-review/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Designer's eye plan review — interactive, like CEO and Eng review. (gstack)\\n- **plan-devex-review** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/plan-devex-review/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Interactive developer experience plan review. (gstack)\\n- **plan-eng-review** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/plan-eng-review/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Eng manager-mode plan review. (gstack)\\n- **plan-tune** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/plan-tune/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Self-tuning question sensitivity + developer psychographic for gstack (v1: observational). (gstack)\\n- **qa** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/qa/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Systematically QA test a web application and fix bugs found. (gstack)\\n- **qa-only** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/qa-only/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Report-only QA testing. (gstack)\\n- **retro** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/retro/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Weekly engineering retrospective. (gstack)\\n- **review** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/review/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Pre-landing PR review. (gstack)\\n- **scrape** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/scrape/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Pull data from a web page. (gstack)\\n- **setup-browser-cookies** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/setup-browser-cookies/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Import cookies from your real Chromium browser into the headless browse session. (gstack)\\n- **setup-deploy** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/setup-deploy/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Configure deployment settings for /land-and-deploy.\\n- **setup-gbrain** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/setup-gbrain/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Set up gbrain for this coding agent: install the CLI, initialize a local PGLite or Supabase brain, register MCP, capture per-remote trust policy. (gstack)\\n- **ship** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/ship/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Ship workflow: detect + merge base branch, run tests, review diff, bump VERSION, update CHANGELOG, commit, push, create PR. (gstack)\\n- **skillify** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/skillify/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Codify the most recent successful /scrape flow into a permanent browser-skill on disk. (gstack)\\n- **spec** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/spec/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Turn vague intent into a precise, executable spec in five phases. (gstack)\\n- **sync-gbrain** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/sync-gbrain/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Keep gbrain current with this repo's code and refresh agent search guidance in CLAUDE.md. Wraps the gstack-gbrain-sync orchestrator with state (gstack)\\n- **built-in-browser** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/built-in-browser/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Read this skill before the first step that uses the built-in browser, the browser pane inside the Claude desktop app (also called the in-app browser, the browser pane, Claude's browser, or \\\\\\\"your own browser\\\\\\\"), whose tools are named mcp__Claude_Browser__* when the session runs in the desktop app and mcp__remote-devices__Claude_Browser__* when a cloud session is linked to the person's computer; before those tools are turned on there may be a single enable__mcp__remote-devices__Claude_Browser tool instead. It covers the pane's persistent sign-ins, tabs and preview_start, reading pages as text, site approvals, what the pane cannot open, and what to do when it cannot be reached. It is not for Claude in Chrome (mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools), which has its own skill, and it does not decide which browser to use.\\n- **chrome-browser** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/chrome-browser/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Read this skill before the first step that uses Claude in Chrome, the browser extension whose tools are named mcp__claude-in-chrome__* (also called Chrome, the browser extension, or the external browser) and which acts in the person's real Chrome with their own sign-ins; before those tools are turned on there may be a single enable__mcp__claude-in-chrome tool instead. It covers loading the tools in one ToolSearch call, checking the person's open tabs and working in a new tab, site permissions, GIF recordings, console logs, dialogs to avoid, and when to stop and ask. It is not for the built-in browser (mcp__Claude_Browser__* or mcp__remote-devices__Claude_Browser__* tools), which has its own skill, and it does not decide which browser to use.\\n- **component-refactoring** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/component-refactoring/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Refactor high-complexity React components in Dify frontend. Use when `pnpm analyze-component --json` shows complexity > 50 or lineCount > 300, when the user asks for code splitting, hook extraction, or complexity reduction, or when `pnpm analyze-component` warns to refactor before testing; avoid for simple/well-structured components, third-party wrappers, or when the user explicitly wants testing without refactoring.\\n- **computer-use** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/computer-use/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Read this skill before the first step of any request to do something in an app on the person's own computer (Notes, Finder, System Settings, any desktop app), to look at their screen, or for \\\\\\\"computer use\\\\\\\". Computer use (desktop control) lets Claude take screenshots of the person's desktop and control it with clicks, typing and scrolling through the Claude desktop app; its tools are named mcp__computer-use__* when the session runs in the desktop app and mcp__remote-devices__computer_* when a cloud session is linked to the person's computer; before computer use is turned on for a conversation there may be no such tools, only an enable__mcp__remote-devices__computer tool, which turns it on. It covers turning it on, picking the right tool, the access flow, and the safety rules for tiered apps, links and financial actions. It is not for websites, which go through Claude in Chrome or the built-in browser and their own skills.\\n- **copy-review** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/copy-review/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Systematic evaluation of marketing copy with scoring rubric and actionable feedback. Use when users request copy review, copy audit, feedback on writing, before/after analysis, conversion optimization review, or want their emails/landing pages/ads/social posts evaluated for clarity, persuasiveness, and engagement.\\n- **copywriter-coach** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/copywriter-coach/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Professional copywriting coach providing instruction, frameworks, and feedback. Use when users ask to write copy, review/audit copy, learn copywriting techniques, improve emails/landing pages/ads/social posts, get feedback on marketing content, or teach copywriting principles like AIDA, PAS, or headline formulas.\\n- **cto-advisor** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/cto-advisor/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Technical leadership guidance for engineering teams, architecture decisions, and technology strategy. Includes tech debt analyzer, team scaling calculator, engineering metrics frameworks, technology evaluation tools, and ADR templates. Use when assessing technical debt, scaling engineering teams, evaluating technologies, making architecture decisions, establishing engineering metrics, or when user mentions CTO, tech debt, technical debt, team scaling, architecture decisions, technology evaluation, engineering metrics, DORA metrics, or technology strategy.\\n- **cto-architecture-decision** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/cto-architecture-decision/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Expert methodology for making and documenting critical architectural decisions using ADRs, trade-off analysis, migration planning, and technical debt management.\\n- **deep-research** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/deep-research/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Use this skill when the user's prompt requires (1) researching a topic across multiple sources, comparing options or alternatives, analyzing trends or history, understanding markets or industries, or reviewing literature or studies and (2) synthesizing that research into a comprehensive, narrative report. If you're planning to search the web or internal knowledge bases, consider using this skill. This skill coordinates research subagents, so use it only when you have a tool for spawning subagents (the Agent or Task tool); otherwise, research the question directly.\\n- **docker-optimization** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/docker-optimization/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Docker image optimization patterns including multi-stage builds, layer caching, security hardening, and size reduction techniques. Use when building Docker images, optimizing container size, improving build performance, or implementing Docker security best practices. Reduces image sizes by 70-90% and build times by 50-80%.\\n- **docs** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/docs/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: docs (editable docs people share and comment on; the default for any document, named as a doc or not: a document, report, proposal, resume, cover letter, letter, contract, policy, form, template, worksheet, essay, handbook, guide, how-to, cheat sheet, SOP or other writing to keep, share, collaborate on, send, submit, print or sign; a doc exports to Word, PDF, Markdown or Google Docs, so needing a file to send, attach, upload, submit or print is no reason to pick Word, and a file nobody asked for is a doc, not Word; a plan, comparison, summary or notes asked in chat stays in chat; a pasted claude.ai artifact link may be a doc: check with docs tools first; Word or another file format named, tracked changes wanted, or a .docx to change or use as a template → that format''s skill): making one → if no docs-connector instructions are in context, call its `guide` (topic.instructions) first; then create the doc (headings only, no body) before any search, file read or plan, even with files attached.\\n- **docx** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/docx/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Use this skill whenever the user wants to create, read, edit, or manipulate Word documents (.docx) or Word templates (.dotx). Triggers include: any mention of Microsoft Word Documents, such as 'Word doc', 'word document', '.docx', '.dotx', 'microsoft doc'. Also use when extracting or reorganizing content from .docx or .dotx files, inserting or replacing images in documents, find-and-replace in Word files, working with tracked changes or comments, or converting content into a polished Word document. If the user asks for a deliverable as a Word or .docx file (to download, email or print), use this skill. However, if they ask for a document, page, report, memo, or notes WITHOUT naming a file format and the session offers Claude's own dedicated document or page skill or connector, use that instead, even if they will email or print it. Do NOT use for PDFs, spreadsheets, Google Docs, or coding unrelated to document generation.\\n- **e2e-testing-patterns** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/e2e-testing-patterns/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Master end-to-end testing with Playwright and Cypress to build reliable test suites that catch bugs, improve confidence, and enable fast deployment. Use when implementing E2E tests, debugging flaky tests, or establishing testing standards.\\n- **email-sequences** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/email-sequences/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Create and optimize email marketing sequences including welcome series, nurture campaigns, sales sequences, and re-engagement emails. Use when users need to write email sequences, create welcome series, optimize email copy, improve email subject lines, design nurture campaigns, or structure product launch emails.\\n- **frontend-design** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/frontend-design/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Create distinctive, production-grade frontend interfaces with high design quality. Use this skill when the user asks to build web components, pages, artifacts, posters, or applications (examples include websites, landing pages, dashboards, React components, HTML/CSS layouts, or when styling/beautifying any web UI). Generates creative, polished code and UI design that avoids generic AI aesthetics.\\n- **git-advanced-workflows** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/git-advanced-workflows/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Master advanced Git workflows including rebasing, cherry-picking, bisect, worktrees, and reflog to maintain clean history and recover from any situation. Use when managing complex Git histories, collaborating on feature branches, or troubleshooting repository issues.\\n- **google-workspace** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/google-workspace/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Read this before the first Google Drive, Docs, Sheets or Slides connector call whenever the task creates or changes a Google file. Use this skill whenever the user wants to create or change a Google Doc, Sheet or Slides file in their Google Drive. Triggers include: a request that names Google Docs, Sheets, Slides or Drive and asks to make, edit, format, copy or rename a file; a docs.google.com link with a request to change that file, even a one-line fix or suggested edits; and any follow-up change to a Google file from earlier in the chat, even \\\\\\\"change it\\\\\\\" or \\\\\\\"add a tab\\\\\\\". Includes helper scripts for document positions, cell ranges and slide layout. However, if the user asks for a doc, deck or spreadsheet without naming Google, or gives a Google file only as source material for something new, use Claude's own output type instead. Do NOT use for read-only questions about a Google file, or for Word, Excel, PowerPoint or PDF files.\\n- **import-memory** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/import-memory/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Import a memory export from another AI assistant into Claude's memory — conversationally, additively, and with the content treated as data.\\n- **landing-pages** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/landing-pages/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Design and optimize high-converting landing pages and sales pages with proven structures and conversion elements. Use when users need to create landing pages, write sales page copy, optimize conversion rates, structure product launch pages, design opt-in pages, or improve page headlines and CTAs.\\n- **linkedin-content-engine** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/linkedin-content-engine/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Complete LinkedIn content creation system for viral posts, lead magnets, case studies, email sequences, and outreach campaigns. Use when creating LinkedIn content strategy, writing posts, developing lead generation funnels, crafting email sequences, or building comprehensive LinkedIn marketing campaigns. Handles everything from single posts to full funnel implementations.\\n- **modern-javascript-patterns** [source: /home/mike/.claude/skills/synced/37867486-c518-4ad6-9a0b-584cb87bc0b1_a871d2f5-5dea-4cbe-bb52-96d42193627b/modern-javascript-patterns/SKILL.md] [trust: trusted]: Master ES6+ features including async/await, destructuring, spread operators, arrow functions, promises, \n... command output truncated for persistence ...\nt ledger = this.objective!;\\\\n 5178 |     const remaining = [...openRequirements(ledger), ...blockedRequirements(ledger)].map((r) => `${r.id} [${r.status}] ${r.text}`);\\\\n 5179 |     const lastFailure = [...ledger.verification].reverse().find((a) => a.status === \\\\\\\"FAILED\\\\\\\" || a.status === \\\\\\\"TIMED_OUT\\\\\\\");\\\\n 5180 |     const failedCheck = turn.verification?.status === \\\\\\\"FAILED\\\\\\\" || turn.verification?.status === \\\\\\\"TIMED_OUT\\\\\\\" ? lastFailure?.failedCheck : undefined;\\\\n 5181 |     const done = turn.execution === \\\\\\\"COMPLETED\\\\\\\" && (turn.outcome === \\\\\\\"VERIFIED\\\\\\\" || turn.outcome === \\\\\\\"NOT_REQUIRED\\\\\\\") && remaining.length === 0;\\\\n 5182 |     const blocked = blockedRequirements(ledger).length > 0 || turn.execution === \\\\\\\"BLOCKED\\\\\\\";\\\\n 5183 |     let nextAction = ledger.nextAction;\\\\n 5184 |     if (!done && !nextAction) {\\\\n 5185 |       if (failedCheck && turn.verification?.command) nextAction = `Fix the failing check (${failedCheck}) and rerun ${turn.verification.command}.`;\\\\n 5186 |       else if (turn.reason === \\\\\\\"STAGNATION_DETECTED\\\\\\\") nextAction = \\\\\\\"Review the repeated action and give the agent new direction (steer or a narrower request).\\\\\\\";\\\\n 5187 |       else if (turn.reason === \\\\\\\"STEP_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED\\\\\\\" || turn.reason === \\\\\\\"TIME_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED\\\\\\\") nextAction = \\\\\\\"Send a follow-up to continue from the saved state, or raise the budget.\\\\\\\";\\\\n 5188 |       else if (remaining.length) nextAction = `Continue with ${remaining[0]}.`;\\\\n 5189 |     }\\\\n 5190 |     return {\\\\n 5191 |       status: done ? \\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\" : blocked ? \\\\\\\"blocked\\\\\\\" : \\\\\\\"incomplete\\\\\\\",\\\\n 5192 |       reason: turn.reason ?? `${turn.execution}/${turn.outcome}`,\\\\n 5193 |       ...(failedCheck ? { failedCheck } : {}),\\\\n 5194 |       remaining,\\\\n 5195 |       ...(nextAction ? { nextAction } : {}),\\\\n 5196 |     };\\\\n 5197 |   }\\\\n 5198 | \\\\n 5199 |   async run(taskPrompt: string | any[], onConfirm?: ConfirmHandler, options?: { signal?: AbortSignal }): Promise<TurnResult> {\\\\n 5200 |     this.ui.banner();\\\\n 5201 |     console.log(`🎯 Task: ${typeof taskPrompt === \\\\\\\"string\\\\\\\" ? taskPrompt : \\\\\\\"[Multi-modal task input]\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\n`);\\\\n 5202 |     console.log(`📁 Workspace: ${this.config.workspace}`);\\\\n 5203 |     console.log(`🐚 Shell: ${this.config.shell}`);\\\\n 5204 |     console.log(`🤖 Model: ${this.config.model} (${this.config.endpoint})\\\\\\\\n`);\\\\n 5205 | \\\\n 5206 |     this.verifiedWorktreeCompletion = false;\\\\n 5207 |     this.activeValidationReceipt = undefined;\\\\n 5208 |     if (this.config.worktree) {\\\\n 5209 |       if (this.activeWorktree) {\\\\n 5210 |         throw new Error(`Cannot start a new task: unresolved worktree ${this.activeWorktree.taskId}; merge or discard it first.`);\\\\n 5211 |       }\\\\n 5212 |       if (!this.worktreeManager.isGitRepo()) {\\\\n 5213 |         throw new Error(`Cannot start worktree-isolated task: ${this.config.workspace} is not a valid git repository.`);\\\\n 5214 |       }\\\\n 5215 |       const configuredSandbox = this.config.sandbox === \\\\\\\"bwrap\\\\\\\" || this.config.sandbox === \\\\\\\"docker\\\\\\\"\\\\n 5216 |         ? this.config.sandbox\\\\n 5217 |         : \\\\\\\"auto\\\\\\\";\\\\n 5218 |       if (!isSandboxAvailable(configuredSandbox)) {\\\\n 5219 |         throw new Error(`Cannot start worktree-isolated task: no usable shell sandbox is available (${configuredSandbox}).`);\\\\n 5220 |       }\\\\n 5221 |       const wt = this.worktreeManager.createWorktree();\\\\n 5222 |       this.activeWorktree = { taskId: wt.taskId, worktreePath: wt.worktreePath, branch: wt.branch };\\\\n 5223 |       this.originalWorkspace = this.config.workspace;\\\\n 5224 |       this.switchWorktreeWorkspace(wt.worktreePath);\\\\n 5225 |       console.log(pc.cyan(`🌲 Spun up speculative git worktree: ${wt.branch} at ${wt.worktreePath}`));\\\\n 5226 |     }\\\\n 5227 | \\\\n 5228 |     if (this.flightRecorder && !this.flightRecorder.isSessionActive()) {\\\\n 5229 |       this.flightRecorder.startSession({\\\\n 5230 |         model: this.config.model,\\\\n 5231 |         endpoint: this.config.endpoint,\\\\n 5232 |         taskPrompt: typeof taskPrompt === \\\\\\\"string\\\\\\\" ? taskPrompt : \\\\\\\"[Multi-modal task input]\\\\\\\",\\\\n 5233 |       });\\\\n 5234 |     }\\\\n 5235 | \\\\n 5236 |     this.resetConversation();\\\\n 5237 |     this.worktreeRunTurnAuthorized = true;\\\\n 5238 |     let turnResult: TurnResult | undefined;\\\\n 5239 |     try {\\\\n 5240 |       if (this.config.tdd) {\\\\n 5241 |         this.config.tdd = false;\\\\n 5242 |         try {\\\\n 5243 |           if (typeof taskPrompt !== \\\\\\\"string\\\\\\\") throw new Error(\\\\\\\"TDD mode does not accept multimodal task input.\\\\\\\");\\\\n 5244 |           const tdd = await this.synthesizer.runAutonomousTdd(this, taskPrompt);\\\\n 5245 |           // The TDD workflow is several turns; report the last turn, downgraded if the workflow itself did not succeed.\\\\n 5246 |           const last = this.lastTurnResult;\\\\n 5247 |           if (last && !tdd.success && last.execution === \\\\\\\"COMPLETED\\\\\\\" && (last.outcome === \\\\\\\"VERIFIED\\\\\\\" || last.outcome === \\\\\\\"NOT_REQUIRED\\\\\\\")) {\\\\n 5248 |             turnResult = deepFreezeTurn(assertTurnResultInvariants({\\\\n 5249 |               ...last, outcome: \\\\\\\"NOT_ESTABLISHED\\\\\\\", reason: \\\\\\\"VERIFIER_FAILED\\\\\\\", detail: `TDD workflow: ${tdd.summary}`, verification: last.verification,\\\\n 5250 |             }));\\\\n 5251 |           } else {\\\\n 5252 |             turnResult = last;\\\\n 5253 |           }\\\\n 5254 |         } finally {\\\\n 5255 |           this.config.tdd = true;\\\\n 5256 |         }\\\\n 5257 |       } else {\\\\n 5258 |         turnResult = await this.runTurn(taskPrompt, onConfirm, options);\\\\n 5259 |       }\\\\n 5260 |     } finally {\\\\n 5261 |       this.worktreeRunTurnAuthorized = false;\\\\n 5262 |       if (this.activeWorktree && this.config.worktree) {\\\\n 5263 |         const hasChanges = this.worktreeManager.hasChanges(this.activeWorktree.taskId);\\\\n 5264 |         if (hasChanges) {\\\\n 5265 |           if (\\\\n 5266 |             this.activeValidationReceipt &&\\\\n 5267 |             !this.worktreeManager.rootHasChanges() &&\\\\n 5268 |             !this.worktreeManager.hasIgnoredChanges(this.activeWorktree.taskId)\\\\n 5269 |           ) {\\\\n 5270 |             console.log(pc.cyan(`🌲 Merging verified changes from worktree ${this.activeWorktree.branch}...`));\\\\n 5271 |             const result = this.mergeActiveWorktree(\\\\n 5272 |               `nexus: apply verified changes from ${this.activeWorktree.taskId}`,\\\\n 5273 |               this.activeValidationReceipt\\\\n 5274 |             );\\\\n 5275 |             if (!result.success) {\\\\n 5276 |               console.log(pc.yellow(`⚠️ Worktree retained because merge was refused: ${result.error || \\\\\\\"unknown error\\\\\\\"}`));\\\\n 5277 |             }\\\\n 5278 |           } else {\\\\n 5279 |             console.log(pc.yellow(`⚠️ Speculative changes are unverified or the root workspace is dirty; retaining worktree at ${this.activeWorktree.worktreePath}`));\\\\n 5280 |           }\\\\n 5281 |         } else {\\\\n 5282 |           this.discardActiveWorktree();\\\\n 5283 |         }\\\\n 5284 |       }\\\\n 5285 |     }\\\\n 5286 |     if (!turnResult) throw new Error(\\\\\\\"Turn ended without a result\\\\\\\");\\\\n 5287 |     return turnResult;\\\\n 5288 |   }\\\\n 5289 | \\\\n 5290 |   /**\\\\n 5291 |    * In-turn delegation for the spawn_subagent tool: a non-durable helper loop whose result is part of the parent's turn\\\\n 5292 |    * (the parent is the durable unit). It has no step allowance: it runs while it progresses (core/execution_tracking.ts)\\\\n 5293 |    * and stops on its report, on the same repetition and stagnation rules as a turn, or when the parent's turn is\\\\n 5294 |    * cancelled. Completed only when the subagent called task_completed or delivered a final report; inference failure,\\\\n 5295 |    * stagnation and cancellation are reported as not completed. Durable autonomous work (/team, nexus operation) does\\\\n 5296 |    * not use this path: it runs as scheduled work units.\\\\n 5297 |    */\\\\n 5298 |   public async runSubagent(prompt: string, onConfirm?: ConfirmHandler, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<SubagentResult> {\\\\n 5299 |     if (this.config.worktree && !this.activeWorktree && !this.inheritedWorktreeScope) {\\\\n 5300 |       throw new Error(\\\\\\\"Cannot run a worktree-enabled subagent without an established worktree authority.\\\\\\\");\\\\n 5301 |     }\\\\n 5302 |     if (this.inheritedWorktreeScope && path.resolve(this.config.workspace) !== path.resolve(this.inheritedWorktreeScope.workspace)) {\\\\n 5303 |       throw new Error(\\\\\\\"Subagent workspace no longer matches its inherited worktree authority.\\\\\\\");\\\\n 5304 |     }\\\\n 5305 |     this.runningAsSubagent = true;\\\\n 5306 |     this.resetConversation();\\\\n 5307 |     this.messages.push({\\\\n 5308 |       role: \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\",\\\\n 5309 |       content: `[Subagent Objective]: ${prompt}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPlease inspect, execute tools as needed, and produce a concise, comprehensive report answering the objective. Call task_completed when done.`,\\\\n 5310 |     });\\\\n 5311 | \\\\n 5312 |     const stagnationSteps = this.config.continuation?.stagnationSteps ?? DEFAULT_CONTINUATION.stagnationSteps;\\\\n 5313 |     const maxRecoveries = this.config.continuation?.maxRecoveries ?? DEFAULT_CONTINUATION.maxRecoveries;\\\\n 5314 |     const tracker = ExecutionTracker.fresh();\\\\n 5315 |     tracker.beginTurn();\\\\n 5316 |     let state = this.workspaceStateIdentity();\\\\n 5317 |     tracker.noteState(state);\\\\n 5318 |     let recoveries = 0;\\\\n 5319 |     let currentStep = 1;\\\\n 5320 |     let finalSummary = \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\";\\\\n 5321 |     let previousReplyTruncated = false;\\\\n 5322 | \\\\n 5323 |     for (;;) {\\\\n 5324 |       if (signal?.aborted) return { completed: false, summary: finalSummary, reason: \\\\\\\"cancelled with the parent turn\\\\\\\" };\\\\n 5325 |       if (tracker.data.stepsWithoutProgress >= stagnationSteps) {\\\\n 5326 |         const loops = tracker.loops().slice(0, 2).map((r) => `${r.action} ×${r.count}`).join(\\\\\\\"; \\\\\\\");\\\\n 5327 |         return { completed: false, summary: finalSummary, reason: `no progress for ${tracker.data.stepsWithoutProgress} consecutive steps${loops ? ` (repeated: ${loops})` : \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"}` };\\\\n 5328 |       }\\\\n 5329 |       const cumulativeStep = tracker.beginStep();\\\\n 5330 |       let assistantMessage: ChatMessage;\\\\n 5331 |       const requestId = randomUUID();\\\\n 5332 |       const { client: activeClient } = this.selectCognitiveClient(currentStep, this.messages);\\\\n 5333 |       try {\\\\n 5334 |         await this.admitWithFindings(activeClient, currentStep, requestId, signal);\\\\n 5335 |         const policy = this.recoveryPolicy ?? recoveryPolicyFromEnv();\\\\n 5336 |         const lengthGuard = { step: currentStep, cutAfterMs: 0 };\\\\n 5337 |         assistantMessage = await new ProviderRecoveryController(policy, this.recoveryClock, currentStep).run(requestId, {\\\\n 5338 |           submit: (s) => this.submitGuardingLength(lengthGuard, this.messages, (request) =>\\\\n 5339 |             activeClient.chatCompletion(request, [...HARNESS_TOOLS, READ_FINDINGS_CAPABILITY.toolDefinition as ToolDefinition], s.requestId, signal)),\\\\n 5340 |           probe: (probeSignal, opts) =>\\\\n 5341 |             typeof activeClient.probeAvailability === \\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\"\\\\n 5342 |               ? activeClient.probeAvailability(probeSignal, { timeoutMs: policy.probeTimeoutMs, ...opts })\\\\n 5343 |               : Promise.resolve({ available: true, condition: \\\\\\\"AVAILABLE\\\\\\\" as const }),\\\\n 5344 |           classify: (err) => (err instanceof InferenceError ? err.toFailure() : null),\\\\n 5345 |           newRequestId: () => randomUUID(),\\\\n 5346 |         }, signal);\\\\n 5347 |       } catch (err: any) {\\\\n 5348 |         if (signal?.aborted) return { completed: false, summary: finalSummary, reason: \\\\\\\"cancelled with the parent turn\\\\\\\" };\\\\n 5349 |         if (err instanceof InferenceError && err.kind === \\\\\\\"output_truncated\\\\\\\" && !previousReplyTruncated) {\\\\n 5350 |           previousReplyTruncated = true;\\\\n 5351 |           tracker.intervention(cumulativeStep, \\\\\\\"output_truncated\\\\\\\", `reply cut off at the output limit: ${err.truncatedCalls.map((c) => c.name).join(\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\") || \\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\"}; nothing ran`);\\\\n 5352 |           this.messages.push({ role: \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\", content: truncatedReplyNotice(err.truncatedCalls, activeClient.getOutputAllowance?.(), `subagent step ${currentStep}`) });\\\\n 5353 |           tracker.evaluateStep(cumulativeStep, { stateBefore: state, stateAfter: state, verdicts: [] });\\\\n 5354 |           currentStep++;\\\\n 5355 |           continue;\\\\n 5356 |         }\\\\n 5357 |         const what = err instanceof ContextBudgetError ? \\\\\\\"context admission refused the request\\\\\\\" : \\\\\\\"inference failed\\\\\\\";\\\\n 5358 |         return { completed: false, summary: finalSummary, reason: `${what}: ${err?.message ?? err}` };\\\\n 5359 |       }\\\\n 5360 | \\\\n 5361 |       previousReplyTruncated = false;\\\\n 5362 |       const parsed = parseModelResponse(assistantMessage, this.getEffectiveTools().map((t) => t.function.name));\\\\n 5363 |       this.messages.push({\\\\n 5364 |         role: \\\\\\\"assistant\\\\\\\",\\\\n 5365 |         content: assistantMessage.content || \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\n 5366 |         ...(parsed.toolCalls.length > 0 ? { tool_calls: parsed.toolCalls } : {}),\\\\n 5367 |       });\\\\n 5368 | \\\\n 5369 |       if (parsed.toolCalls.length === 0) {\\\\n 5370 |         const report = parsed.thoughtText || (typeof assistantMessage.content === \\\\\\\"string\\\\\\\" ? assistantMessage.content : \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");\\\\n 5371 |         if (report.trim()) return this.subagentResult(\\\\\\\"FINAL_TEXT\\\\\\\", report, currentStep);\\\\n 5372 |         return { completed: false, summary: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\", reason: \\\\\\\"the subagent stopped without a report\\\\\\\" };\\\\n 5373 |       }\\\\n 5374 | \\\\n 5375 |       const verdicts: ObservationVerdict[] = [];\\\\n 5376 |       let note: string | undefined;\\\\n 5377 |       let inputState = state;\\\\n 5378 |       for (const call of parsed.toolCalls) {\\\\n 5379 |         const toolName = call.function.name;\\\\n 5380 |         if (signal?.aborted) {\\\\n 5381 |           this.messages.push({ role: \\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\", tool_call_id: call.id, name: toolName, content: \\\\\\\"Cancelled by operator before execution. No action was taken.\\\\\\\" });\\\\n 5382 |           continue;\\\\n 5383 |         }\\\\n 5384 |         const { receipt, shouldExit } = await this.dispatchToolCall(call, currentStep, \\\\\\\"subagent\\\\\\\", onConfirm);\\\\n 5385 | \\\\n 5386 |         if (shouldExit) {\\\\n 5387 |           let args: any = {};\\\\n 5388 |           try {\\\\n 5389 |             args =\\\\n 5390 |               typeof call.function.arguments === \\\\\\\"string\\\\\\\"\\\\n 5391 |                 ? JSON.parse(call.function.arguments)\\\\n 5392 |                 : call.function.arguments;\\\\n 5393 |           } catch {}\\\\n 5394 |           return this.subagentResult(\\\\\\\"TASK_COMPLETED_TOOL\\\\\\\", String(args.summary), currentStep);\\\\n 5395 |         }\\\\n 5396 | \\\\n 5397 |         const verdict = tracker.observe(cumulativeStep, {\\\\n 5398 |           name: toolName,\\\\n 5399 |           args: call.function.arguments,\\\\n 5400 |           success: receipt.success,\\\\n 5401 |           text: receipt.success ? receipt.output ?? \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" : `${receipt.error ?? \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\n${receipt.output ?? \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"}`,\\\\n 5402 |           stateBefore: inputState,\\\\n 5403 |         });\\\\n 5404 |         verdicts.push(verdict);\\\\n 5405 |         if (actionKind(toolName) !== \\\\\\\"observe_workspace\\\\\\\" && actionKind(toolName) !== \\\\\\\"bookkeeping\\\\\\\") inputState = this.workspaceStateIdentity();\\\\n 5406 |         if (verdict.loop) {\\\\n 5407 |           if (recoveries >= maxRecoveries) {\\\\n 5408 |             return { completed: false, summary: finalSummary, reason: `repeated ${toolName} ${verdict.record.count} times with an unchanged result on an unchanged workspace` };\\\\n 5409 |           }\\\\n 5410 |           recoveries++;\\\\n 5411 |           note = `[Nexus recovery ${recoveries}/${maxRecoveries}] You have called ${toolName} with the same arguments ${verdict.record.count} times on an unchanged workspace and received the same result each time. Use the result you have or change approach; if you cannot proceed, report what you found.`;\\\\n 5412 |         }\\\\n 5413 | \\\\n 5414 |         this.messages.push({\\\\n 5415 |           role: \\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\n 5416 |           tool_call_id: call.id,\\\\n 5417 |           name: toolName,\\\\n 5418 |           content: receipt.success\\\\n 5419 |             ? this.toolResultContent(call, receipt.output, currentStep, Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY)\\\\n 5420 |             : `Error executing ${toolName}: ${receipt.error || \\\\\\\"Unknown error\\\\\\\"}`,\\\\n 5421 |         });\\\\n 5422 |       }\\\\n 5423 |       if (note) this.messages.push({ role: \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\", content: note });\\\\n 5424 |       const after = this.workspaceStateIdentity();\\\\n 5425 |       tracker.evaluateStep(cumulativeStep, { stateBefore: state, stateAfter: after, verdicts });\\\\n 5426 |       state = after;\\\\n 5427 | \\\\n 5428 |       finalSummary = parsed.thoughtText || finalSummary;\\\\n 5429 |       currentStep++;\\\\n 5430 |     }\\\\n 5431 |   }\\\\n 5432 | \\\\n 5433 |   /** A subagent that stopped is complete only by the turn predicate (`turnSucceeded`), never by having a report. */\\\\n 5434 |   private subagentResult(modelStop: ModelStop, summary: string, stepsUsed: number): SubagentResult {\\\\n 5435 |     const turn = this.buildTurnResult({ modelStop, finalOutputComplete: true, inferenceFailure: null, interruption: null, limitReached: false, stepsUsed });\\\\n 5436 |     if (turnSucceeded(turn)) return { completed: true, summary };\\\\n 5437 |     return { completed: false, summary, reason: summarizeTurn(turn).line };\\\\n 5438 |   }\\\\n 5439 | }\\\\n\\\",\\n  \\\"end_line\\\": 5439,\\n  \\\"path\\\": \\\"cli/src/agent.ts\\\",\\n  \\\"start_line\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"total_lines\\\": 5439\\n}\"\n./cli/src/index.ts:386:    const { untrustedWorkspaceExtensions } = await import(\"./workspace_trust.js\");\n./cli/src/index.ts:425:    const { untrustedWorkspaceExtensions } = await import(\"./workspace_trust.js\");\n./cli/src/index.ts:444:  .option(\"--remove\", \"Stop trusting the workspace\")\n./cli/src/index.ts:445:  .option(\"--list\", \"List trusted workspaces\")\n./cli/src/index.ts:450:      console.log(list.length ? list.join(\"\\n\") : \"No trusted workspaces.\");\n./cli/src/index.ts:460:    console.log(options.remove ? `No longer trusted: ${real}` : `Trusted: ${real}\\nIts .mcp.json servers and plugins will be loaded (they run code from this workspace). Undo with \\`nexus trust --remove\\`.`);\n./cli/src/agent.ts:146:import { untrustedWorkspaceExtensions } from \"./workspace_trust.js\";\n./cli/src/agent.ts:1345:      const skipped = untrustedWorkspaceExtensions(this.config.workspace, this.config.readOnly);\n./cli/src/workspace_trust.ts:11: * is not trusted (or the session is read-only). Empty when there are none or the workspace is trusted.\n./cli/src/workspace_trust.ts:13:export function untrustedWorkspaceExtensions(workspace: string, readOnly = false): string[] {\n./cli/src/workspace_trust.ts:28:  return path.join(os.homedir(), \".config\", \"nexus\", \"trusted_workspaces.json\");\n./cli/src/workspace_trust.ts:55:/** The trusted workspaces (real paths), as recorded in the trust store. */\n./cli/src/workspace_trust.ts:66:/** Records (or removes) the operator's trust in a workspace. Returns its real path; throws when the store cannot be written. */\n./cli/src/plugins.ts:68:    this.trusted = options?.trusted !== undefined ? options.trusted : isWorkspaceTrusted(this.workspace, this.readOnly);\n./cli/src/security.ts:27:  // The operator's list of trusted workspaces: the agent must not trust a workspace for itself.\n./cli/src/security.ts:28:  \"trusted_workspaces.json\",\n./docs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md:69:| REQ-SEC-002 | EXT-002, TOP_TIER B2 | Workspace MCP configs and plugins load only after `nexus trust`, and never in `--read-only`. | `workspace_trust.ts`; `mcp.ts:509-531`; `plugins.ts:47-76` | TEST + SRC | tested | N | Skills and knowledge are not gated (→ REQ-SEC-113) | ACC-TRUST-04 | K | SPEC-03 |\n./docs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md:119:| REQ-SEC-101 | Audit; DEC-01 | Without opt-in, no model-initiated exec or egress happens without a decision. Untrusted workspace → review; trusted → auto. | safety | `config.ts`, `sidecar.ts:935`, `repl.ts:69`, `index.ts:93,115` | TEST `approval_policy.test.ts`, `tui_surface_e2e.test.ts`, `repl_approval_e2e.test.ts` | tested | ACC-SAFE-01 | **B** | SEC-01, TUI-07 | SPEC-01 / PLAN-01 |\n./docs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md:135:| REQ-SEC-113 | Audit; CTX-001 partial | Workspace skills and knowledge load only when the workspace is trusted. Injected items are annotated with their source. | safety | `skills.ts:31-33`, `knowledge.ts`, `agent.ts:3301` | TEST (SEC-05, AGT-10; test:cli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts::\"ACC-TRUST-04: workspace skills and knowledge load only when workspace is trusted, with source annotations\") | tested | ACC-TRUST-04 | RR | SEC-05, AGT-10 | SPEC-03 / PLAN-03 |\n./docs/v1.0.0/README.md:26:| 03 | Untrusted workspace inputs and egress boundary | [SPEC-03](specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md) | [PLAN-03](plans/PLAN-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md) | M1 | SEC-110, 111, 115, 116 |\n./docs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:776:| **F-04** | Automatic execution of untrusted workspace plugins (Demonstrated) | **EXT-002, EXT-003** | Fully resolved incorporating Operator Decision 2 (policy-based execution authority). |\n./docs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:5:| Status | Ready. Default posture resolved by [DEC-01](../DECISIONS.md#dec-01-default-execution-posture) (option C: review in untrusted workspaces, auto in trusted ones). |\n./docs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:62:   - In an untrusted workspace, the first verifier run requires a one-time approval. The approval shows the exact command and the script body it resolves to (for example the `scripts.test` entry, or the Makefile `test:` recipe).\n./docs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:63:   - In a trusted workspace, they run without asking under the auto posture.\n./docs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:144:- Changing the default for untrusted workspaces from `auto` to review (DEC-01 option C) is a user-visible change. Release notes and the startup banner must state it. Provide `NEXUS_APPROVAL_MODE=auto` and `nexus trust .` + `--yes` for scripted users.\n./docs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:168:| ACC-SAFE-01 | Fresh HOME, `nexus` (TUI) and `nexus repl`, mock gateway scripted to call `run_command \"echo x > ../outside\"`: in an untrusted workspace the call is not executed without an approval panel. After `nexus trust .` it runs without a panel (DEC-01 option C). | PTY |\n./docs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:181:- **DEC-01** is resolved as option C (review in untrusted workspaces, auto in trusted ones). Nothing in this spec is blocked.\n./docs/v1.0.0/plans/PLAN-01-safe-execution-defaults.md:42:| 5 | Route verifier executions through the policy as `exec`. When not trusted and no prior approval exists, emit the approval with `kind: \"verifier\"`, the command, and the resolved script body (read `package.json` `scripts.test`, the Makefile `test:` recipe, `Cargo.toml`/`pytest.ini` presence). Cache the approval per (workspace, command, script digest) for the session. | `agent.ts:1602-1630,3432-3436,4034`; `tester.ts:113-136` (add `describeVerifier()`); `approval_review.ts` (new kind) | 3 |\n./docs/v1.0.0/plans/PLAN-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:1:# PLAN-03: Untrusted workspace inputs and the egress boundary\n./docs/v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md:9:| [DEC-01](#dec-01-default-execution-posture) | Default execution posture | Review by default; auto only in trusted workspaces | No (resolved) |\n./docs/v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md:36:| **C (chosen)** | Posture follows workspace trust. **Untrusted workspace → review** for `write_workspace`, `exec`, `egress` and `context_inject`. **Trusted workspace → auto** for `write_workspace` and `exec`; `egress` asks once per origin per session. `--yes` or `NEXUS_APPROVAL_MODE=auto` force auto. `--sandbox` stays available. | Safe for cloned or unknown repos. Low friction on the operator's own repos after one `nexus trust .`. Reuses the existing trust store, so there is no new concept. |\n./docs/v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md:39:**Rationale.** The threat that matters is content from an untrusted repository or the web. Trust is the existing per-workspace boundary that MCP and plugins already honour. Option C closes SEC-01 and VER-01 on exactly the inputs that cause them, and keeps the daily-driver experience on trusted repos.\n./docs/v1.0.0/AUDIT.md:138:| Tools, security, persistence: `tools, safe_fs, sandbox, security, session, session_export, checkpoint, flight, evidence, git, worktree, mcp, plugins, skills, browser, lsp, file_diff, syntax, diagnostics, workspace_trust`, `core/{authority, resources, durable_file, persistence_protection, capabilities, workspace_path, independent_check, tool_groups}` | 28 | Full read for security-critical files. Grep or skim for session_export, file_diff, syntax, diagnostics, resources, browser, lsp, mcp transports. | C_tools_security_persistence; this file (workspace_trust, workspace_path, tool_groups, independent_check, approval_review, read by lead) | MCP remote transports, browser.ts and lsp.ts got only a skim review: **qualification gap** |\n./docs/reviews/NEXUS_APPLICATION_MATURITY_REVIEW_2026-10-12.md:19:**Maturity gap:** Configuration selection and authorization are not the same thing. Make destination trust explicit, visible before first transmission, policy-controlled per workspace/task, and auditable. Include bulk context and auxiliary requests (embeddings, compaction, delegated agents), and cost ceilings for billable destinations. Treat the provider assessment as a dated snapshot and revalidate source before implementation.\n./docs/reviews/NEXUS_APPLICATION_MATURITY_REVIEW_2026-10-12.md:21:**Acceptance evidence:** negative tests proving untrusted workspace configuration cannot silently redirect context; positive tests for explicit operator approval; end-to-end audit/receipt naming destination/provider/model and data class; enforceable spend/request limits.\n./docs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:1:# SPEC-03: Untrusted workspace inputs and the egress boundary\n./docs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:14:Nexus's threat model, as its docs state it, is that the operator's own configuration (`~/.config/nexus`) holds authority. An opened workspace is data until the operator trusts it (USER_MANUAL §4; `cli/src/workspace_trust.ts`). Workspace MCP configs and plugins honour this: they need `nexus trust` and never run in `--read-only`. Several other inputs that come from the workspace or the network do not:\n./docs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:42:4. **REQ-SEC-113.** Workspace skills and workspace `knowledge.json` are loaded only for trusted workspaces. In an untrusted workspace they are listed by `/skills` as \"untrusted (not loaded)\", and `activate_skill` on one is refused with the `nexus trust` hint.\n./docs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:46:6. **REQ-SEC-115.** Nexus loads plugins only from its own documented directories (`~/.config/nexus/plugins`, `~/.nexus/plugins`, and trusted workspace dirs). `~/.config/opencode/plugins` is removed.\n./docs/NEXUS-CLOUD-PROVIDERS-PLAN.md:55:3. Configuration precedence and project trust: flags, environment, user configuration, workspace configuration, restored sessions. Identify what can currently change a destination or credential source.\n./docs/USER_MANUAL.md:295:- **Workspace Trust**: A workspace's own `.mcp.json` / `mcp.json` servers and `.nexus/plugins/` / `plugins/` modules run code from the workspace, so they are loaded only after you trust it: `nexus trust [path]` (undo with `nexus trust --remove`, list with `nexus trust --list`; recorded in `~/.config/nexus/trusted_workspaces.json`, which the agent's file tools cannot write). Untrusted, they are not loaded and Nexus says so (a notice at start, `nexus doctor`, `nexus mcp`, `nexus plugins`); in `--read-only` mode they are never loaded. Servers and plugins in your home configuration (`~/.config/nexus/`, `~/.nexus/`) are always loaded.\n./docs/PHASE2S_SECURITY_AND_DATA_REVIEW.md:21:4. **Untrusted Workspace Plugin Auto-Execution (F-04):** The current system auto-discovers and imports JavaScript plugins from `workspace/.nexus/plugins` and `workspace/plugins` directly into the host Node.js process using `import()`. Phase 2 defines plugin execution domains as `BOUNDED` or `INDEPENDENT` but fails to explicitly prohibit automatic execution of workspace-controlled extension code, allowing untrusted repositories to achieve immediate host RCE.\n./docs/PHASE2S_SECURITY_AND_DATA_REVIEW.md:330:  5. Phase 2 Section 11 classifies plugins as `INDEPENDENT -> BOUNDED or remain INDEPENDENT`, but fails to forbid automatic execution of untrusted workspace-local code.\n./docs/reviews/NEXUS_TOP_TIER_QUALIFICATION_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-12.md:86:| Q4 | Enforces trustworthy execution boundaries | every mutating/egress capability gated by mode and context; untrusted workspaces cannot execute code; secrets never leave or persist in plaintext |\n./docs/reviews/NEXUS_TOP_TIER_QUALIFICATION_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-12.md:101:| Q4 boundaries | **FAILS** | read-only mode sandboxed; Rust path canonicalisation; TLS never disabled; failover never to a less-trusted endpoint | review-mode bypasses (T-01/T5, T-02/T14, T-06/T11); workspace code execution (T-03); secrets (T-05, T-07, T-08, T10); listing outside workspace (T15); dry-run executes (T13); TOCTOU (T-11); network tools ungated (T-12) |\n./docs/reviews/NEXUS_TOP_TIER_QUALIFICATION_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-12.md:257:| B2 | **Workspace trust**: never import workspace plugins or start workspace MCP servers until the operator trusts that workspace (per-path trust record); `--read-only` disables both; MCP children get an environment allowlist | T-03 | clone a repo containing `plugins/x.mjs` and `.mcp.json` that touch a sentinel → no sentinel without explicit trust, in every mode |\n./docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/trust_inference.md:58:- Expected: workspace-provided executables are not run until the operator trusts that workspace; read-only mode never executes them.\n./docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/trust_inference.md:71:- Acceptance test: R8 rig → `pluginRan:false`, no `MCP_SPAWNED`, and a `notice`/approval asking to trust the workspace; after granting trust\n./docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/peers.md:19:| **4. Operator control / trust** | Sandbox modes `read-only` / `workspace-write` / `danger-full-access`; macOS Seatbelt, Linux bubblewrap (PATH or bundled helper needing unprivileged userns), WSL2 Linux sandbox, native Windows sandbox; `writable_roots`; `sandbox_workspace_write.network_access`; approvals `on-request`/`never`/granular (`untrusted` retired, `on-failure` deprecated); `approvals_reviewer = auto_review` delegates eligible prompts to a reviewer agent without widening the sandbox [CX-SBX][CX-CFG]. `shell_environment_policy` (inherit all/core/none; KEY/SECRET/TOKEN name exclusion when `ignore_default_excludes=false`); project-local config cannot set provider/notify/otel; command + MCP-tool hooks [CX-CFG]. | Modes default(Manual)/acceptEdits/plan/auto(classifier)/dontAsk/bypassPermissions; auto is the starting mode since v2.1.283; deny rules apply in every mode; critical-path `rm` and protected paths never auto-approved; `blockReadsOutsideWorkingDirectories` [CC-PERM]. Bash sandbox: Seatbelt (macOS), bubblewrap+socat+seccomp (Linux/WSL2), network through local proxy with domain allowlist, built on open-source `@anthropic-ai/sandbox-runtime`; file tools, MCP and hooks run outside it; native Windows unsandboxed; unsandboxed retry needs approval [CC-SBX]. Hooks incl. `onFailure:\"block\"` (2.1.295) [CC-CHG]; managed settings, `allowedProviders` pin [CC-GW]. | Per-tool `allow/ask/deny` with glob patterns, last match wins; `external_directory` and `doom_loop` (3 identical calls) default `ask`; `*.env` reads denied by default; `--auto` keeps explicit denies [OC-PERM]. No OS sandbox documented [OC-PERM]. | Modes auto (default per docs) / approve / smart_approve (risk-based) / chat; per-tool Always/Ask/Never in approve & smart modes; first-run mode prompt (v1.54.0); adversary-mode reviewer; prompt-injection detection mentioned [GS-PERM][GS-REL][GS-HOME]. OS sandbox not verified. | No approval/sandbox model documented on pages read; safety relies on git commits + `/undo` [AI-GIT]. |\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":496702509}},"started_at_ms":1792860491820,"completed_at_ms":1792860492321}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-24T16:48:13.280Z","ordinal":297222,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a16ed0-15e0-7941-8950-0ec2aa0b64a4","call_id":"call_ea9a351eca7b40cdbc0bcbeefa4ae836","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.5 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 271038)\n... 35576 bytes omitted ...\n\n41b381ddac182b7f0c783b33df5f1355518f465b\trefs/heads/main\n7d6673f108316dda015b858c85b2abe20f3a4f78\trefs/heads/v1/integration\n79b2f4fab6b9584c4e4e3245a5359f009889b7e2\trefs/tags/v1.0.0-rc.4\nf9d2743decd2dd3a4816f3bd8b5ec7f00d7a8801 refs/heads/codex/pr127-vite-current-main\ne13924963a7ced8a2dadfb007204cf2a8a937ab6 refs/heads/codex/retire-contest-contract-main\ndb1ca558086014912b20dae8232af111ccb21d67 refs/heads/main\nb947ddb43fd3acd67046a553e00037c6a536cf9d refs/heads/v1/docs-dev-pinned-builder-current-main\n519398495b7c3a2e5915ed76eee0bb731feb0bcf refs/heads/v1/integration\n0027ccccb1fbe3bac98bf53a8dd7c1c4bec3e8d3 refs/heads/v1/plan-10-host-preflight-main-1f3c655\n41df6a74745187585bb129013effdd3b173d78ae refs/heads/v1/plan-c1-post-contest-maintenance\n132719cf3e8fac735427820c764a560e4fc77a97 refs/heads/v1/rc3-candidate-main\n43c3d884a028f286443f7e8fd6d7a5fd4c947c83 refs/heads/v1/rc4-notes-completeness\nf13f9ef979ccf7a324a7af236357e035dcd08007 refs/heads/v1/rc4-notes-security-updates\n0cff3c3c142c01b837d03f37006d76deae7ead1e refs/heads/v1/rc4-version-sync\n41b381ddac182b7f0c783b33df5f1355518f465b refs/heads/v1/rc5-version-sync\nbe2cd2f255414f554572c86baa884d9271ff5ea1 refs/heads/v1/readiness-rc4-refresh\nda64255de37f3bab8ccdc5fed48bbeedf1a70b88 refs/heads/v1/startup-workspace-trust\nb14b314fab6dbf57b3ad029f5b8848fdff72fabe refs/heads/v1/startup-workspace-trust-rebased\n./cli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts:11:import { isWorkspaceTrusted, listTrustedWorkspaces, setWorkspaceTrusted, untrustedWorkspaceExtensions } from \"../src/workspace_trust.js\";\n./cli/test/workspace_trust.test.ts:68:      const store = path.join(home, \".config\", \"nexus\", \"trusted_workspaces.json\");\n./cli/test/doctor.test.ts:259:      // Untrusted: the workspace's own configuration is still inspected, and reported as not loaded.\n./cli/test/doctor.test.ts:263:      assert.match(find(untrusted, \"mcp.config\")!.evidence.join(\"\\n\"), /\\.mcp\\.json: not loaded — this workspace is not trusted\\. Trust it with `nexus trust/);\n./cli/test/doctor.test.ts:265:      assert.match(find(untrusted, \"plugins.config\")!.evidence.join(\"\\n\"), /p\\.mjs: not loaded — this workspace is not trusted/);\n./nexus-session-session_20261023_134635_z1dnlfek.json:41:      \"independenceDetail\": \"The workspace-resolved verifier has no matching pre-change baseline. Its current output cannot establish verification; start a fresh task from an operator-reviewed trusted workspace baseline.\",\n./nexus-session-session_20261023_134635_z1dnlfek.json:161:      \"content\": \"You are Nexus Code, an autonomous AI software engineering agent running in a Linux shell environment.\\nYou solve coding problems, build features, fix bugs, and refactor codebases by iteratively exploring, reading, editing, and verifying files.\\n\\n### Operating Rules:\\n1. EXPLORE FIRST: Before editing files, always explore the workspace using `file_tree`, `list_dir`, `find_files`, `search_code`, or `read_file`. For large files, use `outline_file` first to inspect class/function signatures without loading the entire file into context.\\n2. HIGH-EFFICIENCY BATCHING: Prefer `batch_read` to read multiple files concurrently in a single turn, and `file_info` to inspect metadata without loading whole files.\\n3. PRECISE EDITS & PATCHES: When modifying existing files, use `edit_file` with unique snippets or `patch_file` with unified diffs.\\n4. PLANNING: Use `update_plan` only for work with three or more distinct phases (not for a fix and its test); update it alongside other tool calls.\\n5. VERIFICATION: After making code changes, use `run_command` with bash/zsh commands to run tests, builds, or linting (e.g. `cargo check`, `cargo test`, `npm test`, `pytest`).\\n6. SELF-CORRECTION: If a command or tool returns an error, analyze the error message, identify the root cause, and fix it.\\n7. AUTONOMOUS COMPLETION: Keep executing tools until the entire task is thoroughly resolved. When everything is verified and done, invoke `task_completed` with a short summary of what changed and how it was checked.\\n8. BOUNDED CONTEXT: Your context window is limited, and earlier tool output is compacted out of the conversation as you work; your own messages are what carry forward. After inspecting large output you will rely on later, state its key findings in a line or two (with file and symbol references) before moving on; do not restate small files or narrate routine steps. Re-read earlier output only when you need a specific detail you did not record or it may have changed.\\n\\n\\n\\nAlways explain your reasoning before or alongside your tool calls.\\n\\n### Shell Environment:\\n- Active Shell: bash (standard GNU/Linux shell environment)\\n- Workspace directory: /home/mike/nexus-code\\n- Test command (Nexus verifies with it): `make test`\\n\\n### Project Instructions (loaded from AGENTS.md):\\n# Project Guidelines & Agent Instructions\\n\\n## Commands\\n- Build: `make build` or `cargo build` or `npm run build`\\n- Test: `make test` or `cargo test` or `npm test`\\n- Lint: `cargo clippy` or `npm run lint`\\n\\n## Code Conventions\\n- Maintain documentation integrity and keep existing comments.\\n- Always run tests after making changes to verify correctness.\\n- Keep functions modular and clearly typed.\\n\\n## v1.0.0 work and concurrent agents\\n- The v1.0.0 plan is in `docs/v1.0.0/` (start at `docs/v1.0.0/README.md`). Implement one claimed PR slice at a time, within the safe change boundary in its plan.\\n- When more than one agent may be working, follow `docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md`. In short:\\n  - Claim the slice atomically under `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/`.\\n  - Use one worktree and branch per slice; never edit `~/nexus-code` directly.\\n  - Isolate `HOME` for every test or probe run.\\n  - Claim hot files before editing them.\\n  - Take the `GATEWAY` claim for any inference run.\\n  - Never use `pkill -f`.\\n  - Check `git log` for foreign commits before every commit.\\n  - Leave push, tag and publish to the owner.\\n\\n### Specialized Agent Skills Available:\\nYo…259152 tokens truncated… this: they need `nexus trust` and never run in `--read-only`. Several other inputs that come from the workspace or the network do not:\n./docs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:42:4. **REQ-SEC-113.** Workspace skills and workspace `knowledge.json` are loaded only for trusted workspaces. In an untrusted workspace they are listed by `/skills` as \"untrusted (not loaded)\", and `activate_skill` on one is refused with the `nexus trust` hint.\n./docs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md:46:6. **REQ-SEC-115.** Nexus loads plugins only from its own documented directories (`~/.config/nexus/plugins`, `~/.nexus/plugins`, and trusted workspace dirs). `~/.config/opencode/plugins` is removed.\n./docs/NEXUS-CLOUD-PROVIDERS-PLAN.md:55:3. Configuration precedence and project trust: flags, environment, user configuration, workspace configuration, restored sessions. Identify what can currently change a destination or credential source.\n./docs/USER_MANUAL.md:295:- **Workspace Trust**: A workspace's own `.mcp.json` / `mcp.json` servers and `.nexus/plugins/` / `plugins/` modules run code from the workspace, so they are loaded only after you trust it: `nexus trust [path]` (undo with `nexus trust --remove`, list with `nexus trust --list`; recorded in `~/.config/nexus/trusted_workspaces.json`, which the agent's file tools cannot write). Untrusted, they are not loaded and Nexus says so (a notice at start, `nexus doctor`, `nexus mcp`, `nexus plugins`); in `--read-only` mode they are never loaded. Servers and plugins in your home configuration (`~/.config/nexus/`, `~/.nexus/`) are always loaded.\n./docs/PHASE2S_SECURITY_AND_DATA_REVIEW.md:21:4. **Untrusted Workspace Plugin Auto-Execution (F-04):** The current system auto-discovers and imports JavaScript plugins from `workspace/.nexus/plugins` and `workspace/plugins` directly into the host Node.js process using `import()`. Phase 2 defines plugin execution domains as `BOUNDED` or `INDEPENDENT` but fails to explicitly prohibit automatic execution of workspace-controlled extension code, allowing untrusted repositories to achieve immediate host RCE.\n./docs/PHASE2S_SECURITY_AND_DATA_REVIEW.md:330:  5. Phase 2 Section 11 classifies plugins as `INDEPENDENT -> BOUNDED or remain INDEPENDENT`, but fails to forbid automatic execution of untrusted workspace-local code.\n./docs/reviews/NEXUS_TOP_TIER_QUALIFICATION_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-12.md:86:| Q4 | Enforces trustworthy execution boundaries | every mutating/egress capability gated by mode and context; untrusted workspaces cannot execute code; secrets never leave or persist in plaintext |\n./docs/reviews/NEXUS_TOP_TIER_QUALIFICATION_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-12.md:101:| Q4 boundaries | **FAILS** | read-only mode sandboxed; Rust path canonicalisation; TLS never disabled; failover never to a less-trusted endpoint | review-mode bypasses (T-01/T5, T-02/T14, T-06/T11); workspace code execution (T-03); secrets (T-05, T-07, T-08, T10); listing outside workspace (T15); dry-run executes (T13); TOCTOU (T-11); network tools ungated (T-12) |\n./docs/reviews/NEXUS_TOP_TIER_QUALIFICATION_ASSESSMENT_2026-10-12.md:257:| B2 | **Workspace trust**: never import workspace plugins or start workspace MCP servers until the operator trusts that workspace (per-path trust record); `--read-only` disables both; MCP children get an environment allowlist | T-03 | clone a repo containing `plugins/x.mjs` and `.mcp.json` that touch a sentinel → no sentinel without explicit trust, in every mode |\n./docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/trust_inference.md:58:- Expected: workspace-provided executables are not run until the operator trusts that workspace; read-only mode never executes them.\n./docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/trust_inference.md:71:- Acceptance test: R8 rig → `pluginRan:false`, no `MCP_SPAWNED`, and a `notice`/approval asking to trust the workspace; after granting trust\n./docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/peers.md:19:| **4. Operator control / trust** | Sandbox modes `read-only` / `workspace-write` / `danger-full-access`; macOS Seatbelt, Linux bubblewrap (PATH or bundled helper needing unprivileged userns), WSL2 Linux sandbox, native Windows sandbox; `writable_roots`; `sandbox_workspace_write.network_access`; approvals `on-request`/`never`/granular (`untrusted` retired, `on-failure` deprecated); `approvals_reviewer = auto_review` delegates eligible prompts to a reviewer agent without widening the sandbox [CX-SBX][CX-CFG]. `shell_environment_policy` (inherit all/core/none; KEY/SECRET/TOKEN name exclusion when `ignore_default_excludes=false`); project-local config cannot set provider/notify/otel; command + MCP-tool hooks [CX-CFG]. | Modes default(Manual)/acceptEdits/plan/auto(classifier)/dontAsk/bypassPermissions; auto is the starting mode since v2.1.283; deny rules apply in every mode; critical-path `rm` and protected paths never auto-approved; `blockReadsOutsideWorkingDirectories` [CC-PERM]. Bash sandbox: Seatbelt (macOS), bubblewrap+socat+seccomp (Linux/WSL2), network through local proxy with domain allowlist, built on open-source `@anthropic-ai/sandbox-runtime`; file tools, MCP and hooks run outside it; native Windows unsandboxed; unsandboxed retry needs approval [CC-SBX]. Hooks incl. `onFailure:\"block\"` (2.1.295) [CC-CHG]; managed settings, `allowedProviders` pin [CC-GW]. | Per-tool `allow/ask/deny` with glob patterns, last match wins; `external_directory` and `doom_loop` (3 identical calls) default `ask`; `*.env` reads denied by default; `--auto` keeps explicit denies [OC-PERM]. No OS sandbox documented [OC-PERM]. | Modes auto (default per docs) / approve / smart_approve (risk-based) / chat; per-tool Always/Ask/Never in approve & smart modes; first-run mode prompt (v1.54.0); adversary-mode reviewer; prompt-injection detection mentioned [GS-PERM][GS-REL][GS-HOME]. OS sandbox not verified. | No approval/sandbox model documented on pages read; safety relies on git commits + `/undo` [AI-GIT]. |\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a16ec6-5068-7a43-a634-88dec514377c","create_time":1792860493.2800689}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
