{"timestamp":"2026-10-24T18:12:18.958Z","ordinal":300949,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a16f1d-138e-7e82-a78b-5fa05b7eab37","author":"/root/plan05_readonly_review","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/plan05_readonly_review\nPayload:\n**CHANGES_REQUIRED** for `7c993d4c1321c0ddd54868e309628cc588417465` against base `676abb371c8c91970af6d920d5c516497e3ccdef`; reviewed diff SHA-256 `b10d57ca01b7d026bb611202b7cae83eb616e78cb02d01bdbe58da0171c3ff9c`.\n\nThe file-byte read is capped, but [cst.rs](/home/mike/nexus-code/core/src/cst.rs:70) copies every line into an owned `String` in `Vec<String>`. An 8 MiB newline-dense file can therefore allocate millions of `String` headers—roughly 192 MiB for the vector alone, before allocator overhead and the source text. That leaves a practical memory-exhaustion path through a file within the stated limit. The evidence note’s claim that peak memory is only “somewhat above” file size understates this amplification ([README.md](/home/mike/nexus-code/docs/reviews/evidence/v1/DEFECT-CST-UNBOUNDED-20261024/README.md:48)).\n\nAvoid retaining an owned string per line, or add a substantially lower line-count/memory bound, and cover a newline-dense input in a regression. I did not run tests."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a16f1a-0d89-7262-a71e-eb3a6dfcc08e","create_time":1792865538.958083}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":15353}}
